window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2018"] = {"id": "jammu-2018", "title": "University of Jammu 2018", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology Entrance Test 2018, booklet code FDM-2542-A (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · 2018 · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "A group in which hypothesized cause is present is called:", "options": {"a": "Control Group", "b": "Experimental Group", "c": "Random Group", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the group in which the hypothesised cause is present is the experimental group. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable (IV), the supposed cause or treatment, and measures its effect on a dependent variable (DV). Participants who receive the IV form the experimental group. Participants treated in exactly the same way but without the IV form the control group, which shows what would have happened anyway and serves as the baseline for comparison. Ronald Fisher (The Design of Experiments, 1935) made random assignment central to this logic: chance, not the researcher, decides who goes into which group, so the groups are equal at the start and any later difference between them can be put down to the IV. Example: Arjun wants to know whether background music improves recall. Students randomly assigned to study with music are the experimental group; those who study the same list in silence are the control group. Option b is right because the experimental group is, by definition, the one exposed to the hypothesised cause. Exam trap: examiners offer 'random group' because random assignment is part of every true experiment; but randomness describes how people are allocated to groups, not which group receives the treatment.", "topic": "Experimental and control groups", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Dr. John is interested in how environmental rewards affect helping behaviour in child. Dr. John will closely identify with ______ perspective.", "options": {"a": "Behavioural", "b": "Psychodynamic", "c": "Humanistic", "d": "Neuroscience"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a researcher who studies how environmental rewards affect children's helping works within the behavioural perspective. This perspective explains behaviour by observable events in the environment: stimuli, rewards, punishments and the learning they produce. John B. Watson (1913) launched it with his manifesto 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', arguing that psychology should study only observable behaviour, not the mind. B. F. Skinner (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938) then showed how consequences shape behaviour through operant conditioning: a response followed by reinforcement becomes more likely to occur again. A behaviourist would therefore ask which rewards (praise, stickers, attention) make helping more frequent, and how they should be delivered. Example: six-year-old Arjun is praised by his teacher each time he helps a classmate pick up dropped books, and over the following weeks he helps more often; the behaviourist explains this as learning from consequences, with no need to look inside his mind. Option a is right because environmental rewards and the behaviour they strengthen are exactly the subject matter of the behavioural perspective. Exam trap: 'helping' sounds warm and humanistic, so some students pick c; but the stem's telling words are 'environmental rewards', and rewards point straight to behaviourism.", "topic": "Behavioural perspective", "fig": ""}, {"q": "______ perspective suggests that people have little control over their lives. The ______ perspective suggests people do have control over their lives.", "options": {"a": "Cognitive/Neuroscience", "b": "Neuroscience/Behavioural", "c": "Psychodynamic/Humanistic", "d": "Humanistic/Behavioural"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the psychodynamic perspective suggests that people have little control over their lives, while the humanistic perspective suggests that they do have control. This is the free will versus determinism issue, one of the major issues on which introductory textbooks (for example Robert Feldman's Understanding Psychology) compare the major perspectives. Determinism holds that behaviour is caused by forces outside our conscious choice. The psychodynamic view of Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900) is strongly deterministic: by psychic determinism no act is accidental, and behaviour is driven by unconscious urges and conflicts shaped in early childhood. The humanistic perspective of Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954), the 'third force' of the 1950s and 1960s, stresses free will, personal choice and each person's innate push towards growth and self-actualisation. The other perspectives also take sides: the behavioural and neuroscience views are deterministic (environment or biology causes behaviour), and the cognitive view leans towards free will. Example: Meera keeps choosing friends who treat her badly. A psychodynamic therapist sees an unconscious repetition of a childhood pattern; a humanistic therapist believes Meera can become aware of her choices and choose differently. Option c is right because it pairs the most deterministic perspective with the one most committed to free will, in the order the blanks require. Exam trap: d names a real contrast but reverses it, putting the free-will perspective in the 'little control' blank.", "topic": "Free will versus determinism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In comparison to experiments done in psychological laboratories, experiments done in natural settings are likely to have:", "options": {"a": "Fewer problems in exerting experimental control about the same number of problems", "b": "More problems in exerting experimental control about the same number of problems", "c": "No problems", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: experiments done in natural settings (field experiments) are likely to have more problems in exerting experimental control than laboratory experiments. In a laboratory experiment the researcher creates exactly the conditions wanted, holds extraneous variables constant, assigns participants at random and measures the dependent variable precisely; this gives high internal validity, that is, confidence that the IV caused the change. A field experiment still manipulates an IV, but in a real setting such as a train, a school or a street. There the researcher cannot control noise, crowds, who happens to pass by or what else is going on; random assignment is often hard, and measurement is rougher. In exchange, field experiments have higher ecological (external) validity, and participants often do not know they are being studied, so demand characteristics are fewer. A classic example is the study by Irving Piliavin, Judith Rodin and Jane Piliavin (1969), who staged a collapse on New York subway trains to see who would help. Example: Kabir tests whether a polite sign reduces littering by placing it in some parks and not others; weather, crowds and park staff all vary in ways he cannot control. Option b is right because loss of control is the main cost of moving an experiment out of the laboratory. Exam trap: a reverses the trade-off; the laboratory, not the field, gives easier control. Key note: options a and b both end with a stray phrase, 'about the same number of problems', probably a leftover second clause from the source test bank; read the options on their first half, and b stands.", "topic": "Field versus laboratory experiments", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Skinnerian Operant Conditioning theory, a negative reinforcement is:", "options": {"a": "Nothing but punishment", "b": "A bio-feedback", "c": "A withdrawing or removal of positive reinforcer", "d": "An application of aversive stimuli"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the older teaching-aptitude wording this item copies, negative reinforcement is described as the withdrawal or removal of a positive reinforcer. To understand the term properly, start with operant conditioning as B. F. Skinner (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938; Science and Human Behavior, 1953) set it out and as textbooks now tabulate it: every consequence is classified by asking two questions. First, does the consequence make the behaviour more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. That gives four cells. Positive reinforcement: something pleasant, a positive reinforcer, is added (praise for finished homework), so the behaviour increases. Negative reinforcement: something aversive is removed (or avoided), so the behaviour increases; this covers escape and avoidance learning. Example: Riya takes a painkiller and her headache stops, so she reaches for a painkiller again the next time. Positive punishment: something aversive is added (a scolding), so the behaviour decreases. Negative punishment: something pleasant is removed (time-out, response cost, a fine), so the behaviour decreases. Option c is right because it is the definition this item is built on, and it is the only option that links 'negative' to removing something rather than to applying pain. Exam trap: the commonest confusion is to treat negative reinforcement as punishment; all reinforcement, positive or negative, increases behaviour. Key note: c is the answer this item expects, following the older teaching-aptitude wording; strictly, though, removing a positive reinforcer is negative punishment and adding an aversive stimulus (option d) is positive punishment, so no option gives the modern textbook definition of negative reinforcement (removal of an aversive stimulus); Skinner himself (1953) counted removing a positive reinforcer as punishment. The answer used here stands.", "topic": "Negative reinforcement", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "What term is used to refer to the idea that the stimulation of the CR by the CS will gradually wear off over time?", "options": {"a": "Stimulus generalization", "b": "Unconditioning", "c": "Spontaneous recovery", "d": "Extinction"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: extinction is the gradual weakening and disappearance of a conditioned response (CR) when the conditioned stimulus (CS) is presented again and again without the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). Ivan Pavlov (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927) showed it with dogs. Once a bell (CS) had been paired with food (UCS), the bell alone made the dog salivate (CR). If the bell then sounded repeatedly with no food, salivation shrank trial by trial until it stopped. So 'wear off over time' in the stem means across CS-alone trials, not the mere passing of time; a CS that is never presented does not extinguish. Extinction is not erasure. After a rest, the CS can bring the CR back in weaker form, which Pavlov called spontaneous recovery, and this shows that extinction is new inhibitory learning laid over the old association rather than its removal. Example: Meera's phone ringtone (CS) used to come before stressful calls from her boss, so it made her tense (CR). After she changes jobs and hears the same tone many times with no bad news, the tension fades: that is extinction. Option d is right because it is the standard term for the CR weakening when the CS is no longer followed by the UCS. Exam trap: spontaneous recovery sounds like a time-based effect, but it is the return of the CR after extinction, the opposite of fading.", "topic": "Extinction in classical conditioning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sensory register has all the following characteristics except:", "options": {"a": "Visual information lasts about quarter of a second", "b": "It holds an exact image of each sensory experience", "c": "Auditory information lasts about 4 seconds", "d": "Capacity is 7 ± 2 bits of information"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: a capacity of 7 plus or minus 2 items belongs to short-term memory, not to the sensory register. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information passes from the sensory register to short-term memory and then to long-term memory. The sensory register holds a brief, literal copy of whatever reaches the senses: an exact, unprocessed image or sound. Its capacity is very large, but the trace fades within moments unless attention passes it on. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory. George Sperling (1960) revealed the size of iconic memory with his partial-report method: people saw a grid of 12 letters for 50 milliseconds and could report any one row cued by a tone just afterwards, so they must briefly have held most of the letters (about 9 of the 12); the image lasts about a quarter to half a second. Echoic memory lasts longer, about 2 to 4 seconds (Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder, 1972). Example: Kabir is not listening when his mother calls, yet a moment later he can 'replay' her last words; that is echoic memory at work. Option d is right because the limit of 7 plus or minus 2 chunks comes from George Miller's (1956) paper on short-term memory. Exam trap: the stem says 'except', so a, b and c, which are all true of the sensory register, are the distractors.", "topic": "Sensory register", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Wechsler has defined intelligence as a global capacity because it characterises the individual's behaviour:", "options": {"a": "Throughout the world", "b": "As a capacity to learn", "c": "As a whole", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: David Wechsler called intelligence a global capacity because it characterises the individual's behaviour as a whole. Wechsler (The Measurement of Adult Intelligence, 1939; usually quoted from the 1944 edition) gave the most quoted definition of intelligence: 'the aggregate or global capacity of the individual to act purposefully, to think rationally and to deal effectively with his environment'. He explained both adjectives. Intelligence is global because it describes the person's behaviour as a whole, not one isolated skill. It is aggregate because it is made up of separate abilities which, though not entirely independent, can be told apart. This is why his tests give subtest scores (vocabulary, block design and so on) that combine into one full-scale IQ. He also replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), since mental age stops rising in adulthood. His scales began with the Wechsler-Bellevue (1939) and continued with the WISC (1949), the WAIS (1955) and the WPPSI (1967). Example: Meera's verbal, spatial and memory subtest scores differ, but together they describe how she copes with the world as a whole, which is her global capacity. Option c is right because 'as a whole' is Wechsler's own reason for the word 'global'. Exam trap: 'aggregate' and 'global' are easily swapped; aggregate refers to the many separate abilities, global to the whole person's behaviour.", "topic": "Wechsler's definition of intelligence", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "Which of the following suggests that an emotion is produced when an event or object is perceived by the thalamus, which conveys this information simultaneously to the cerebral cortex, skeletal muscles and the autonomic nervous system?", "options": {"a": "Opponent-Process theory", "b": "Cannon-Bard theory", "c": "James-Lange theory", "d": "Cognitive-Appraisal theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Cannon-Bard theory holds that the thalamus relays an emotional stimulus at the same time to the cerebral cortex and to the body. Walter Cannon (1927) put it forward as an alternative to the James-Lange theory, and Philip Bard (1928) supported it with brain studies in cats. In the theory, the thalamus sends one signal upward to the cortex, which produces the conscious feeling, and another downward to the skeletal muscles and the autonomic nervous system, which produce arousal. Feeling and bodily change therefore happen together and independently; neither causes the other. Cannon's objections to James-Lange were that visceral changes are too slow to explain quick emotions, that the same changes (a racing heart, sweating) occur in very different emotions and in fever or exercise, and that animals whose viscera were cut off from the brain still showed emotion. Later research placed the main relay in the hypothalamus and limbic system rather than the thalamus, but the idea of simultaneous, independent signals is the theory's core. Example: Arjun sees a snake on the path; on this view his fear and his pounding heart arise at the same moment. Option b is right because a thalamic signal sent simultaneously to cortex and body is the defining claim of Cannon-Bard. Exam trap: c, James-Lange, is the theory Cannon attacked; it puts bodily change first and feeling second, the reverse of 'simultaneously'.", "topic": "Cannon-Bard theory of emotion", "fig": "Walter Cannon and Philip Bard"}, {"q": "The desire to perform a behaviour in order to obtain an external reward such as praise, grades or money is known as:", "options": {"a": "Extrinsic motivation", "b": "Intrinsic motivation", "c": "Achievement motivation", "d": "Overjustification"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the desire to perform a behaviour to obtain an external reward such as praise, grades or money is extrinsic motivation. Motivation is extrinsic when the reason for acting lies outside the activity: a reward to gain or a punishment to avoid. It is intrinsic when the activity is rewarding in itself, done for interest, enjoyment or challenge. Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), in self-determination theory, place these on a continuum and argue that people thrive when three needs are met: autonomy, competence and relatedness. External rewards can backfire. Mark Lepper, David Greene and Richard Nisbett (1973) offered preschoolers who already liked drawing a 'Good Player' certificate for drawing; the children who expected the reward later drew less in free play than children who got no reward or an unexpected one. This is the overjustification effect: an expected external reward leads people to explain their behaviour by the reward, which undermines the interest that was already there. Example: Riya studies chemistry to win the phone her parents have promised (extrinsic), while her friend Meera studies it because she loves puzzling out reactions (intrinsic). Option a is right because the stem describes acting for a reward that lies outside the task. Exam trap: d, overjustification, also involves external rewards, but it names what rewards do to intrinsic interest, not the motive itself.", "topic": "Extrinsic motivation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The need to reduce a drive such as hunger through appropriate objects or individuals, thereby filling some crucial lack within the organism is known as:", "options": {"a": "Deficit motive", "b": "Growth motive", "c": "D-Cognition", "d": "D-Love"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: a motive that reduces a drive such as hunger through appropriate objects or people, filling a crucial lack in the organism, is a deficiency (deficit) motive. Abraham Maslow (Toward a Psychology of Being, 1962) divided human motives into two kinds. Deficiency motives, or D-motives, arise from something missing: food, safety, belonging, esteem. They push the person to fill the gap, they are satisfied by appropriate things or people from outside (a meal, a protector, a friend), and once satisfied they stop motivating. Growth motives, or B-motives (from 'being'), push towards self-actualisation and the being values such as truth, beauty and wholeness; they are not used up in the same way but grow stronger as they are pursued. Maslow applied the same D and B labels to perception and to love. D-cognition is perception distorted by one's needs, seeing others mainly as means of satisfying them, whereas B-cognition sees a person or object as it is. D-love is deficiency love, needing another to fill one's own gap, whereas B-love is unselfish love of the other's being. Example: hungry after a long exam, Kabir can think of nothing except lunch; once he eats, the urge disappears, a classic deficiency motive. Option a is right because filling a lack through outside objects or people is Maslow's definition of deficiency motivation; 'deficit motive' is a variant wording of the same term. Exam trap: c and d also begin with 'D', for deficiency, but they name kinds of perception and love, not kinds of motive.", "topic": "Maslow's deficiency motives", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "______ is a motivated state caused by physiological deprivation such as lack of food or water.", "options": {"a": "Need", "b": "Instinct", "c": "D-Drive", "d": "Homeostasis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the motivated state caused by physiological deprivation is a drive, which Clark Hull (1943) wrote as D. In Hull's drive-reduction theory (Principles of Behavior, 1943), a need is the physiological deprivation itself: the body's tissues lack water or food. A drive is the aroused, tense, energising state that the need produces, and it pushes the animal to act. Any response that reduces the drive is reinforced and so learned. Hull wrote drive as D in his equation for reaction potential, in which habit strength is multiplied by drive: a well-learned habit is performed only when a drive is present to energise it. Drive reduction restores homeostasis, the body's tendency to keep a stable internal balance, a term Walter Cannon coined and set out in The Wisdom of the Body (1932). Example: after a day of fasting, Arjun's body lacks food (the need); he feels restless and driven to find something to eat (the drive); eating reduces the drive, and the route he took to the canteen is reinforced. Option c is right because the stem describes the motivated state, not the deprivation, and that state is Hull's drive, D. Exam trap: a, need, tempts because the stem mentions deprivation; but the need is the lack itself, and the motivated state it causes is the drive. Key note: some introductory texts use 'need' loosely for this state, and 'D-Drive' is an unusual label (read it as Hull's drive, D); but a need is the deprivation itself and the motivated state it causes is a drive, so c stands.", "topic": "Hull's drive", "fig": "Clark Hull"}, {"q": "The unconscious perception of stimuli that are too weak to exceed the absolute threshold for detection is known as:", "options": {"a": "Just noticeable difference", "b": "Differential threshold", "c": "Subliminal perception", "d": "Response bias"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: subliminal perception is the unconscious perception of stimuli too weak or brief to exceed the absolute threshold for detection. 'Limen' is Latin for threshold, so subliminal means below the threshold. The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus a person detects 50 per cent of the time (Gustav Fechner, 1860). A subliminal stimulus is not consciously reported, yet it can still be processed. The best evidence comes from masked priming: Anthony Marcel (1983) flashed words so briefly, followed by a pattern mask, that people could not say what they had seen, yet an unseen word still speeded their responses to a related word. Such effects are real but small and short-lived. The famous claim of James Vicary (1957) that flashing 'Eat popcorn' and 'Drink Coca-Cola' during a film raised cinema sales was a hoax that he admitted in 1962, and there is no good evidence that subliminal advertising changes what people buy. Example: Riya sees a smiling face for a few milliseconds, instantly masked; she reports seeing nothing, but then rates an unfamiliar Chinese character a little more favourably. Option c is right because the stem defines subliminal perception exactly. Exam trap: a and b are one and the same thing, the just noticeable difference or difference threshold (Ernst Weber, 1834), which concerns noticing a change between two stimuli, not perceiving a stimulus below the absolute threshold.", "topic": "Subliminal perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A motion that is produced most simply by flashing a light in darkness and then, in few milliseconds later, flashing another light near the location of the first light is called:", "options": {"a": "Stroboscopic motion", "b": "Real motion", "c": "Selective adaptation", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: when one light is flashed in darkness and another is flashed nearby a few milliseconds later, people see a single light moving from the first place to the second; this is stroboscopic motion, a form of apparent motion. Nothing actually moves. The brain takes two still stimuli, separated by the right interval, and fills in the path between them. The effect depends on timing: with an interval below about 30 milliseconds the two lights look simultaneous, with roughly 30 to 200 milliseconds they look like one moving light, and with longer intervals they look like two lights flashing in turn. Max Wertheimer's (1912) study of such apparent movement, the phi phenomenon, showed that the perceived motion lies not in either stimulus but in the relation between them, and it launched Gestalt psychology. Films work the same way, with 24 still frames a second seen as smooth movement, and so do the chasing lights of neon signs. Example: Meera watches the bulbs around a cinema hoarding switch on one after another and sees a ring of light racing round, though each bulb only blinks in place. Option a is right because the stem describes the standard laboratory demonstration of stroboscopic motion. Exam trap: b, real motion, is what the experience feels like, but real motion needs an object that actually moves, its image sweeping across the retina.", "topic": "Stroboscopic motion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The last stage of creative thinking is:", "options": {"a": "Verification", "b": "Evaluation", "c": "Incubation", "d": "Preparation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the classic model of creative thinking, verification is the last stage. Graham Wallas (The Art of Thought, 1926), drawing on the accounts of famous scientists and mathematicians, described four stages. Preparation: the problem is defined and studied consciously, information is gathered and solutions are tried. Incubation: the problem is set aside, and work seems to continue outside awareness. Illumination: the solution appears suddenly, the 'aha' moment of insight. Verification: the idea is tested, checked and worked out in detail to see whether it really solves the problem. The order is fixed: preparation, incubation, illumination, verification. Incubation is the stage most studied since, because it explains why a break so often helps. Example: Kabir struggles for days with a design for his science project (preparation), gives up and goes to a cricket match (incubation), suddenly sees the answer on the bus home (illumination), and then builds and tests a model to make sure it works (verification). Option a is right because testing and refining the insight is the final step in Wallas's sequence. Exam trap: b, evaluation, sounds like a natural last step, and it appears in Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's (1996) five-step account (preparation, incubation, insight, evaluation, elaboration); but there it is fourth, followed by elaboration, so it is not the last stage in either model.", "topic": "Wallas's stages of creativity", "fig": "Graham Wallas"}, {"q": "Proximity, continuity and closure are all forms of:", "options": {"a": "Figures", "b": "Ground", "c": "Grouping", "d": "Figure-ground"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: proximity, continuity and closure are Gestalt laws of perceptual grouping, the principles by which we see separate elements as belonging together. The Gestalt psychologists held that the whole is different from the sum of its parts, and Max Wertheimer (1923) set out the grouping laws. Proximity: elements close together are grouped, so dots spaced in pairs look like pairs. Similarity: elements alike in colour, shape or size are grouped. Continuity, or good continuation: we follow smooth lines and curves rather than abrupt changes of direction. Closure: we fill gaps to see complete figures, so a broken circle is still seen as a circle. Common fate: elements moving together are grouped. All of them serve Prägnanz, the tendency to see the simplest, most stable organisation. Figure-ground organisation, shown by Edgar Rubin (1915) with his vase that can also be seen as two faces, is a separate and more basic process: splitting the scene into an object (the figure) and its background (the ground). Example: Riya sees a row of streetlights curving round a bend as one continuous line (continuity) and a logo made of broken arcs as a complete circle (closure). Option c is right because proximity, continuity and closure are all principles of grouping. Exam trap: d, figure-ground, is the other famous Gestalt principle of organisation, so it lures students who remember 'Gestalt' but not which process these laws belong to.", "topic": "Gestalt grouping laws", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If central tendency is found by using whole population as input data then this is classified as:", "options": {"a": "Sample statistic", "b": "Population statistic", "c": "Population tendency", "d": "Population parameter"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a measure of central tendency computed from the whole population is a population parameter. A population is every member of the group a researcher wants to describe, such as all college students in a state; a sample is a smaller set drawn from it. A number that describes a population is a parameter; the same number calculated from a sample is a statistic. Parameters are written with Greek letters (mu for the population mean, sigma for its standard deviation), and statistics with Roman letters (x-bar or M for the sample mean, s for its standard deviation). A parameter is fixed but usually unknown, because whole populations are rarely measured; a statistic is known but varies from sample to sample, and it is used to estimate the parameter. That estimation is the work of inferential statistics. A quick memory aid: Population goes with Parameter, Sample goes with Statistic. Example: if Arjun records the age of every one of the 60 employees in his small firm and finds a mean of 34 years, that mean is a population parameter; if he measured only 15 of them, their mean would be a sample statistic. Option d is right because a value based on the whole population is by definition a parameter. Exam trap: b, 'population statistic', mixes the two words; a statistic always comes from a sample, so the phrase is not a standard term.", "topic": "Parameter versus statistic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following is used to compare the variation or dispersion in two or more sets of data even though they are measured in different units?", "options": {"a": "Range", "b": "Standard deviation", "c": "Coefficient of variation", "d": "Mean deviation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the coefficient of variation (CV) is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even when they are measured in different units. Karl Pearson (1896) defined it as the standard deviation expressed as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Because the SD and the mean are in the same units, the units cancel and the CV is a pure percentage. That allows comparisons the SD cannot make: heights in centimetres against weights in kilograms, or the spread of marks in a class whose mean is 30 against one whose mean is 80. The set with the lower CV is the more consistent (more uniform, less variable). Example: in Riya's class, height has a mean of 160 cm and an SD of 8 cm (CV = 5 per cent), while weight has a mean of 50 kg and an SD of 5 kg (CV = 10 per cent); weight varies relatively twice as much, even though 8 looks larger than 5. Because it divides by the mean, the CV strictly needs ratio data with a true zero. Option c is right because it is the standard relative, unit-free measure of dispersion. Exam trap: the standard deviation is the best-known measure of spread, so it attracts guesses; but it is stated in the original units, so comparing an SD in centimetres with one in kilograms is meaningless.", "topic": "Coefficient of variation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "For a positively skewed distribution, mean is always:", "options": {"a": "Less than the median", "b": "Less than the mode", "c": "Greater than the mode", "d": "Difficult to tell"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: in a positively skewed distribution the mean is greater than the mode. Skewness is lack of symmetry. In positive (right) skew most scores are low and a few very high scores form a long tail to the right; income in a city is the classic case. The three averages then separate. The mode stays at the peak, where most scores lie. The median, which depends only on position, moves a little towards the tail. The mean uses the value of every score, so the few large values pull it furthest into the tail. The order is therefore mean greater than median greater than mode. In negative (left) skew the order reverses. Karl Pearson (1895) noted that in moderately skewed distributions the mode is roughly 3 times the median minus 2 times the mean, and his coefficient of skewness, (mean minus mode) divided by the standard deviation, often written 3 times (mean minus median) divided by the standard deviation, is positive in right skew. Example: in Riya's town most families earn modest amounts, but a few business owners earn a great deal; the mean income is higher than the median and much higher than the mode. Option c is right because the long right tail drags the mean above the mode. Exam trap: a, 'less than the median', is true of negative skew; remember that the mean is pulled towards the tail. Key note: 'always' holds for the usual single-peaked distribution; Paul von Hippel (2005) showed exceptions in some discrete or multimodal distributions, but the textbook rule is what this item tests, so c stands.", "topic": "Skewness and the averages", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If most repeated observations recorded are outliers of data then mode is considered as:", "options": {"a": "Intended measure", "b": "Poor measure", "c": "Percentage measure", "d": "Best measure"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: if the most repeated observations are outliers, the mode is a poor measure of central tendency. The mode is simply the most frequent value in a set of data. Its job, like that of any average, is to represent the typical score. Usually the mode sits at the peak of the distribution, where most scores cluster, and it has real strengths: it is easy to find, it is not pulled about by a few extreme scores, and it is the only average for nominal data (the most common blood group, the most popular brand). But the mode looks only at frequency, not at where the value lies. If the value that repeats most often is an outlier, a value far from the bulk of the data, the mode lands away from the centre and misrepresents the typical score. The median, which depends on the middle position, would then be the better choice. Example: in a class test, 6 of Kabir's 30 classmates were absent and recorded as 0, while the other 24 scored between 50 and 80, each mark appearing only once or twice; the mode is 0, which describes almost nobody. Option b is right because a mode located at an outlier fails to describe the centre of the data. Exam trap: textbooks say the mode is unaffected by extreme values, which tempts students towards d; that is true when extremes are rare, but here the extreme values are the most frequent ones.", "topic": "Limits of the mode", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychoanalytic theory is often NOT criticised for which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Being unfalsifiable", "b": "Being unscientific", "c": "Being deterministic", "d": "Being simplistic"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychoanalytic theory is not usually criticised for being simplistic; if anything, critics call it too elaborate. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) built a large, layered system: id, ego and superego, five psychosexual stages, the Oedipus complex, a long list of defences and a code of dream symbols. The standard criticisms run in other directions. First, it is unfalsifiable: the philosopher Karl Popper (Conjectures and Refutations, 1963) argued that no possible observation could count against it. If Arjun loves his mother, that is Oedipal; if he hates her, he is defending against Oedipal love. A theory that explains every outcome predicts none. Second, it is unscientific: the data came from a small, unrepresentative group of Viennese patients, written up from memory after sessions, with no controls and no independent check. Third, it is deterministic: psychic determinism holds that every act, slip and dream is caused by unconscious forces, which leaves little room for free will. Critics also call it sexist (Karen Horney attacked the idea of penis envy in the 1920s) and say it overstresses sex and early childhood. Option d is right because 'simplistic' is the one label missing from the standard list; the theory is attacked for its complexity, not for being too simple. Exam trap: the stem asks which criticism is NOT made, so the three familiar criticisms (a, b and c) are the distractors. Key note: some critics call Freud reductionist for tracing much of behaviour back to sexual drives and early childhood, which is a kind of oversimplification; but 'simplistic' is not on the standard list of criticisms, so d stands.", "topic": "Criticisms of psychoanalysis", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which Freudian defense mechanism does this statement illustrate, \"I am not snobbish, you are\"?", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Sublimation", "c": "Repression", "d": "Denial"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: saying 'I am not snobbish, you are' is projection, attributing one's own unacceptable trait or impulse to someone else. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) described defence mechanisms as unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety, and his daughter Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) catalogued them. In projection the impulse is not removed; it is relocated. The person still notices the snobbery, anger or jealousy, but sees it in someone else, which feels far less threatening than owning it. The logic runs: 'I do not hate him; he hates me.' Example: Meera secretly envies her colleague's promotion, but tells her friends that the colleague has always been jealous of her. Projection in a broader sense also lies behind the projective hypothesis of tests such as the Rorschach: people read their own needs and conflicts into ambiguous stimuli. Option a is right because the speaker disowns a trait (snobbishness) and pins it on the listener, which is the defining move of projection. Exam trap: denial also begins with 'I am not', but denial refuses to accept a painful fact of reality ('my exam result cannot be a fail'); it does not shift a trait onto another person. A quick test: if the unwanted trait ends up located in someone else, it is projection.", "topic": "Projection defence mechanism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What proportion of variability in most personality traits tend to be heritable?", "options": {"a": "20%", "b": "40%", "c": "60%", "d": "80%"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: about 40 percent (roughly 40 to 50 percent) of the variation in most personality traits is heritable. Heritability is the proportion of the differences among people in a trait, within one population at one time, that can be traced to genetic differences. Behaviour genetics estimates it with two natural experiments. Twin studies compare identical (monozygotic) twins, who share all their genes, with fraternal (dizygotic) twins, who share about half; adoption studies compare children with their biological and adoptive parents. Thomas Bouchard's Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart (from 1979; main report 1990) found that identical twins raised in different homes were about as alike in personality as those raised together, putting heritability at about 0.40 to 0.50. Kerry Jang, John Livesley and Philip Vernon (1996) found roughly 40 to 60 percent for the Big Five. The surprise was that shared family environment adds almost nothing; most of the rest is non-shared environment, such as different friends, teachers and experiences (Robert Plomin and Denise Daniels, 1987). Example: Riya and her identical twin Siya, raised in different cities, are both outgoing, yet differ in their interests because of their own experiences. Option b is right because textbooks summarise the heritability of most personality traits as about 40 percent. Exam trap: 60 percent is the top of some single-trait estimates, and 80 percent mixes personality up with traits such as height or adult IQ; for most personality traits the summary figure is about 40 percent. Key note: estimates range from about 40 to 50 percent, with some studies up to 60, and introductory texts usually say 'about 40 percent' or '40 to 50 percent', so b stands.", "topic": "Heritability of personality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What according to Rogers is the cause of all psychological problems?", "options": {"a": "Blocks in our actualising tendency", "b": "Faulty learning", "c": "Emotional disturbance", "d": "Impairment in self-awareness"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: for Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), psychological problems arise when the actualising tendency, the innate drive to grow and fulfil one's potential, is blocked. Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951; his formal statement of the theory, 1959) held that every organism moves towards growth. As the self develops, a child needs positive regard. When love is conditional ('we love you when you top the class'), the child takes in conditions of worth and starts valuing experiences by whether they win approval, not by whether they feel right. Experiences that do not fit the self-concept are then denied or distorted. The gap between the self-concept and actual experience is incongruence, felt as tension, anxiety and confusion. Incongruence blocks the actualising tendency, and that block is what Rogers saw behind psychological disturbance. Example: Arjun was praised only for his marks and told that boys who cry are weak; as an adult he denies his sadness, feels empty and anxious, and cannot follow his love of music. Therapy removes the block by offering unconditional positive regard, empathy and genuineness, so that the growth tendency can work again. Option a is right because Rogers traced all disturbance to conditions of worth and incongruence that stop the actualising tendency. Exam trap: 'emotional disturbance' sounds like a cause but is only a description of the problem, and 'faulty learning' is the behaviourists' explanation, not the humanists'.", "topic": "Rogers: blocked actualising tendency", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Behaviour motivated by objections to the belief that society regards men as superior to women is called:", "options": {"a": "Neglect", "b": "Superiority feelings", "c": "Inferiority feelings", "d": "Masculine protest"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) called this masculine protest, a striving for power and superiority that, in women, takes the form of protest against the inferior feminine role that society assigns them. Adler first used the term in 1910 and developed it in The Neurotic Constitution (1912). His starting point was inferiority. In Study of Organ Inferiority (1907) he argued that a weak organ drives a person to compensate. He then widened the idea: every child begins small and dependent, so inferiority feelings are universal, and the effort to overcome them drives all striving. In a culture that treats men as strong and women as weak, being 'feminine' becomes a symbol of inferiority, and masculine protest is the reaction: refusing that inferior position and pushing for power and equality. Adler saw the belief in male superiority as a cultural prejudice, not a biological fact, and thought it harmed both sexes. He later folded the idea into the broader striving for superiority. Example: Meera, told that girls should not study engineering, works furiously to beat every boy in her class and refuses any task labelled 'women's work'. Option d is right because the stem describes behaviour driven by objection to the belief that men are superior, which is Adler's masculine protest. Exam trap: inferiority and superiority feelings are also Adler's terms, so every option sounds Adlerian; but they are general motives found in everyone, not the specific reaction against male superiority.", "topic": "Adler's masculine protest", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Personality psychologists such as Cattell, Spearman, Eysenck all used factor analysis in their work, as they felt this was more objective method of developing trait taxonomies. However this method is criticised for which of the following reasons?", "options": {"a": "Choosing factor names", "b": "Identify how many factors exist", "c": "Choosing terms to enter into the analysis", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: factor analysis is criticised on all three counts: the researcher chooses what goes in, decides how many factors to keep, and names them. Factor analysis is a statistical method that takes the correlations among many measures and finds a few underlying factors that explain them. Charles Spearman (1904) invented it to show a general intelligence factor, g. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) applied it to trait words and ratings and arrived at 16 source traits (the 16PF, 1949); Hans Eysenck (1916 to 1997) arrived at three superfactors (extraversion, neuroticism, psychoticism); later researchers found five. The disagreement comes from three points of judgement. (1) Input: the result depends on which variables or trait terms enter the analysis; leave out a domain and no factor for it can appear (garbage in, garbage out). (2) Number of factors: how many to keep is a decision, guided by rules such as Henry Kaiser's eigenvalue-greater-than-one rule or Cattell's scree test (1966), and different rules give different answers. (3) Naming: a factor is only a cluster of loadings; calling it 'anxiety' or 'neuroticism' is the researcher's interpretation. Example: Kabir factor-analyses 40 adjectives; if he leaves out every word about honesty, no honesty factor can emerge, however important honesty is in real life. Option d is right because a, b and c are all standard criticisms of the method. Exam trap: each single option is true, so a student who spots one valid criticism may stop there; with three true statements, 'All of the above' is the answer.", "topic": "Criticisms of factor analysis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When motives change to self-sustaining interests, Allport would say that they have become:", "options": {"a": "Functionally autonomous", "b": "Extinct", "c": "Needs", "d": "Habituated"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Gordon Allport (1897 to 1967) said that motives which have changed into self-sustaining interests are functionally autonomous. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation (1937) Allport argued against explaining every adult motive by tracing it back to childhood drives or to past rewards. A motive may begin as a means to an end, but over time it can become independent of the need that first produced it and continue for its own sake. His example: a sailor first goes to sea to earn a living, but after he becomes rich he still craves the sea. Allport distinguished two levels. Perseverative functional autonomy covers low-level routines that keep going without reward, such as habits, rituals and addictions. Propriate functional autonomy covers higher motives tied to the self (the proprium): values, interests and life goals, which are central to mature adult motivation. Example: Riya began practising the veena only to escape her parents' scolding; now, at thirty, she plays for hours with no one asking her to. Option a is right because a motive that has turned into a self-sustaining interest, cut off from its original cause, is exactly what Allport meant by functional autonomy. Exam trap: 'habituated' sounds like 'habit', but habituation is a fall in response to a repeated stimulus; a functionally autonomous motive keeps going strongly, it does not fade.", "topic": "Allport's functional autonomy", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Studying groups of people in order to discover general principles concerning human behaviour:", "options": {"a": "Nomothetic approach", "b": "Idiographic approach", "c": "Proprium", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: studying groups of people to discover general principles of behaviour is the nomothetic approach. The terms come from the German philosopher Wilhelm Windelband (1894), who divided the sciences into those that seek general laws (nomothetic, from Greek nomos, law) and those that study unique events (idiographic, from Greek idios, one's own). Gordon Allport (1937) brought the distinction into personality psychology. Nomothetic research measures many people on the same dimensions, compares them and looks for principles that hold for everyone: how extraversion relates to sociability, or whether anxiety rises before exams. Most trait research is nomothetic; Raymond Cattell (16PF, 1949) and Hans Eysenck (1916 to 1997) worked this way, using group statistics and factor analysis. Idiographic research studies one person in depth to grasp his or her unique pattern, as Allport did in Letters from Jenny (1965), his analysis of some 300 letters written by one woman. Example: Arjun gives a personality questionnaire to 2,000 college students to find out whether conscientiousness predicts marks; that is nomothetic. His friend Meera spends a year interviewing one artist to understand her life; that is idiographic. Option a is right because the stem describes group study aimed at general principles, which is the nomothetic approach. Exam trap: the two Greek-rooted terms are easy to swap; remember nomothetic as 'laws for all' and idiographic as 'the idiosyncratic individual'.", "topic": "Nomothetic versus idiographic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Comorbidity explains the notion that:", "options": {"a": "People suffering with the same psychopathology die at the same time", "b": "Sufferers exhibit a chronic fear of death", "c": "Depression always happens in winter", "d": "Differentially defined disorders can co-occur"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: comorbidity is the notion that differently defined disorders can occur together in the same person. In psychiatry, comorbidity means the presence of two or more distinct disorders at the same time, such as major depressive disorder with generalised anxiety disorder, or alcohol use disorder with post-traumatic stress disorder. In mental health it is the rule rather than the exception. The National Comorbidity Survey of Ronald Kessler and colleagues (1994) in the United States found that more than half of all lifetime disorders occurred in the roughly one person in seven who had a history of three or more disorders. Comorbidity matters for three reasons. It complicates diagnosis, because symptoms overlap. It worsens prognosis, since comorbid cases are usually more severe and harder to treat. And it raises questions about classification: if anxiety and depression so often travel together, perhaps they share causes, which is one reason for transdiagnostic treatments. Example: Meera is diagnosed with panic disorder and, a year later, also meets the criteria for major depression; her clinician has to plan treatment for both, not just one. Option d is right because the co-occurrence of separately defined disorders in one individual is exactly what comorbidity means. Exam trap: 'morbidity' sounds like 'mortality', so examiners offer options about dying or death; comorbidity is about illnesses co-existing in a living person, and it is also different from prevalence, which is how common a disorder is in a population.", "topic": "Comorbidity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not used to define psychopathology?", "options": {"a": "Deviation from the statistical norm", "b": "Deviation from the sexual norm", "c": "Deviation from the social norm", "d": "Maladaptive behaviour"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: deviation from the sexual norm is not one of the general criteria used to define psychopathology. No single test separates normal from abnormal, so clinicians weigh several criteria together. (1) Statistical infrequency: behaviour far from the average, at the tail of the normal curve; its flaw is that rare talent, such as a genius IQ, is also deviant. (2) Deviation from social norms: behaviour that breaks a society's explicit or implicit rules; its flaw is that norms differ across cultures and eras. (3) Maladaptive behaviour, or dysfunction: behaviour that stops a person working, studying or keeping relationships. (4) Personal distress. Ronald Comer's widely used textbook summary is the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Marie Jahoda (1958) offered a positive picture of ideal mental health, and Jerome Wakefield (1992) defined disorder as harmful dysfunction. A separate 'sexual norm' is not on any of these lists, and history shows why it would be dangerous: homosexuality was treated as a disorder until the American Psychiatric Association removed it from the DSM in 1973. Example: Kabir washes his hands for three hours a day; that is unusual (statistical), distressing and stops him working (maladaptive), whatever anyone's sexual norms. Option b is right because a, c and d are standard criteria and b is not. Exam trap: 'social norm' and 'sexual norm' differ by only a few letters, and students who read quickly treat them as the same criterion.", "topic": "Criteria of abnormality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following has been called the \"common cold\" of psychopathology because it occurs so frequently and because almost everyone has experienced it at some time?", "options": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "b": "Bi-polar disorder", "c": "Unipolar disorder", "d": "Paranoid schizophrenia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: unipolar depression has been called the 'common cold' of psychopathology because it is so common and because nearly everyone has felt low at some point. The nickname is usually credited to Martin Seligman, writing on learned helplessness in the 1970s; it is also quoted as 'the common cold of psychiatry'. Unipolar means one pole: the person has episodes of depression only, with no mania or hypomania. In DSM-5 this is major depressive disorder: depressed mood or loss of interest plus other symptoms (changes in sleep and appetite, fatigue, worthlessness, poor concentration, thoughts of death) for at least two weeks. Bipolar disorder swings between two poles, depression and mania. The nickname fits the numbers. Lifetime prevalence of major depression is about 15 to 20 percent, and after puberty women are affected about twice as often as men. By contrast bipolar I disorder and schizophrenia each affect about 1 percent, and OCD about 2 percent. The second half of the stem matters too: ordinary sadness after loss or failure is universal, so almost everyone knows a mild version of depression, while few people know mania or psychosis from the inside. Example: Meera, after failing an entrance exam, has felt hopeless, sleepless and uninterested in friends for a month, with no periods of elation. Option c is right because unipolar depression is both the most common of these disorders and the one closest to ordinary experience. Exam trap: 'bipolar' also involves depression and sounds like the bigger category, but bipolar disorder is far rarer and its defining feature is mania.", "topic": "Depression as common cold", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Type of personality disorder characterized by always having to be the center of attention:", "options": {"a": "Borderline", "b": "Anti-social", "c": "Paranoid", "d": "Histrionic"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: needing always to be the centre of attention is the hallmark of histrionic personality disorder. In DSM-5 it belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic group (with antisocial, borderline and narcissistic personality disorders). It is a pervasive pattern of excessive emotionality and attention seeking, beginning by early adulthood and shown by at least five of eight features. The first listed is being uncomfortable in situations where one is not the centre of attention. The others are inappropriately seductive or provocative behaviour, rapidly shifting and shallow emotions, using physical appearance to draw attention, speech that is impressionistic and lacking in detail, theatrical and exaggerated emotional expression, suggestibility, and seeing relationships as more intimate than they really are. The name comes from the Latin histrio, actor, and the diagnosis grew out of the older idea of the 'hysterical personality'. Example: Riya arrives at a cousin's wedding in a striking outfit, tells a dramatic story with tears and laughter, and sulks when attention turns to the bride; she calls a man she met once her 'closest friend'. Option d is right because constant attention seeking is the core, and first, criterion of histrionic personality disorder. Exam trap: narcissistic personality disorder also craves attention, but the narcissist wants admiration for being superior, while the histrionic person wants attention of any kind, even through drama or illness; borderline is the Cluster B neighbour that examiners most often swap in.", "topic": "Histrionic personality disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Increased dopamine neurotransmitter receptors in the brain could be responsible for the onset of:", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "Anxiety", "d": "Phobia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: increased dopamine receptors, and too much dopamine activity in general, are linked to schizophrenia. This is the dopamine hypothesis, the best-known biochemical theory of the disorder. Arvid Carlsson (Nobel Prize, 2000) showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter in its own right. The hypothesis rests mainly on drug evidence. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) found that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, calmed psychotic patients, and Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) argued that it works by blocking dopamine receptors. In the mid-1970s the laboratories of Philip Seeman and Solomon Snyder showed that the clinical potency of the typical antipsychotics closely tracks how strongly they bind to D2 receptors. In the other direction, amphetamine, which raises dopamine activity, can produce a paranoid psychosis in healthy people. Post-mortem and PET studies reported increased D2 receptor density in some patients. The revised version says that excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations), while a shortage in the mesocortical pathway contributes to negative and cognitive symptoms. Example: Kabir, after weeks of heavy amphetamine use, began hearing voices and believing he was being followed, a drug-induced picture that mimics paranoid schizophrenia. Option b is right because excess dopamine activity at its receptors is the classic biochemical account of schizophrenia. Exam trap: depression is also explained by neurotransmitters, so students pick it; but the classic theory of depression is a shortage of monoamines (noradrenaline and serotonin), the opposite direction.", "topic": "Dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is culture bound disorder?", "options": {"a": "A disorder specific to a particular cultural context", "b": "A disorder that is bound to occur in most cultures", "c": "A disorder not included in DSM's diagnostic categories", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: a culture-bound disorder (culture-bound syndrome) is a pattern of distress and behaviour that is recognised mainly within a particular cultural context. The term was introduced by the Hong Kong psychiatrist Pow Meng Yap in the 1960s for syndromes that seemed tied to local beliefs. Each has its own name, explanation and expected course in that culture. Examples: dhat syndrome in South Asia, anxiety, weakness and fatigue blamed on loss of semen, named by N. N. Wig (1960); koro in South and East Asia, the fear that the genitals are shrinking into the body; amok in Malaysia, brooding followed by a sudden outburst of violence; ataque de nervios in Latin America; and taijin kyofusho in Japan, fear of offending others by one's appearance or manner. DSM-IV (1994) listed such syndromes in a glossary in its appendix. DSM-5 (2013) renamed them cultural concepts of distress, because all distress is shaped by culture, and added the Cultural Formulation Interview to help clinicians ask about it. Example: Arjun, 19, from a village in Bihar, complains of weakness and poor memory and is sure it is because semen is passing in his urine; a clinician who knows only Western categories may miss that this is dhat. Option a is right because the defining feature of these syndromes is that they belong to a specific cultural setting. Exam trap: option b sounds similar but says the opposite; a disorder found in most cultures is universal, not culture-bound.", "topic": "Culture-bound syndromes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The most common focus of obsessive thoughts is:", "options": {"a": "Sexual impulses", "b": "Aggressive impulses", "c": "Dirt and contamination", "d": "Repeated doubts"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: fear of dirt and contamination is the most common focus of obsessive thoughts. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety; compulsions are repetitive acts or mental acts performed to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. They come in recognisable themes. In a large clinical study of about 560 patients with OCD, Steven Rasmussen and Jane Eisen (1992) found contamination obsessions in about 50 percent, pathological doubt in about 42 percent, somatic obsessions in 33, need for symmetry in 32, aggressive obsessions in 31 and sexual obsessions in 24. Most patients had more than one theme. Each theme tends to pair with a compulsion: contamination with washing and cleaning, doubt with checking, symmetry with ordering and counting. Later research grouped the symptoms into similar dimensions: contamination and washing, harm and checking, symmetry and ordering, and taboo (aggressive, sexual, religious) thoughts. Example: Riya, after touching a door handle, fears she will pass deadly germs to her mother and washes her hands until they are raw. Option c is right because contamination is the single most frequent obsession in clinical samples. Exam trap: repeated doubts (checking the stove or the lock) are also very common and come a close second, which makes d the tempting near miss. Key note: pathological doubt comes a close second in the Rasmussen and Eisen study, but contamination leads, so c stands.", "topic": "Common OCD obsessions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a criterion for PTSD?", "options": {"a": "Three or more dissociative symptoms", "b": "Re-experiencing the event", "c": "Emotional numbing and detachment", "d": "Exaggerated startle response"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'three or more dissociative symptoms' is not a criterion for PTSD; it was the DSM-IV requirement for acute stress disorder. Both disorders follow exposure to a traumatic event involving actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence. DSM-5 (2013) defines PTSD by four symptom clusters lasting more than one month: (1) intrusion, re-experiencing the event through memories, nightmares or flashbacks; (2) avoidance of reminders; (3) negative alterations in cognition and mood, including detachment from others, emotional numbing and inability to feel positive emotions; and (4) alterations in arousal and reactivity, such as hypervigilance, irritability and an exaggerated startle response. DSM-5 offers an optional 'with dissociative symptoms' specifier (depersonalisation or derealisation), but dissociation is not required. Acute stress disorder covers the first days to weeks after trauma. In DSM-IV it lasted 2 days to 4 weeks and required at least three of five dissociative symptoms: numbing or detachment, reduced awareness of surroundings, derealisation, depersonalisation and dissociative amnesia. In DSM-5 it lasts 3 days to 1 month and needs 9 of 14 symptoms from any category. Example: Kabir survived a train accident; three months later he has nightmares, avoids stations, feels cut off from his family and jumps at every loud noise. That is PTSD, with no dissociation needed. Option a is right because a required count of dissociative symptoms belongs to acute stress disorder, not PTSD. Exam trap: emotional numbing (c) sounds dissociative, so students mark it as the odd one out; but numbing is a PTSD symptom, listed under negative alterations in cognition and mood.", "topic": "PTSD versus acute stress disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "CBT is generally perceived as:", "options": {"a": "Phenomenological", "b": "Psychodynamic", "c": "Humanistic", "d": "Evidence based"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is generally seen as evidence based. CBT combines the cognitive therapies, the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (from 1955) and the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (from the 1960s), with the techniques of behaviour therapy. It holds that distorted thoughts keep distressing emotions and unhelpful behaviour going, and it treats them by identifying, testing and changing those thoughts, alongside behavioural methods such as exposure and activity scheduling. Several features made it easy to test: it is structured, time-limited and problem-focused, often follows a manual, and tracks progress with standard scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory. As a result it is the most researched psychotherapy. Dianne Chambless and Steven Hollon (1998) set criteria for empirically supported treatments, and CBT for depression and several anxiety disorders met them. Andrew Butler, Jason Chapman, Evan Forman and Aaron Beck (2006) reviewed 16 meta-analyses and found large effects for depression, generalised anxiety, panic, social phobia and PTSD. In the UK, NICE guidelines recommend it as a first-line treatment for depression and most anxiety disorders. Example: Arjun, who panics before presentations, keeps a thought record, tests his prediction that everyone will laugh, and his anxiety falls over 12 sessions while a weekly rating scale tracks the change. Option d is right because CBT's reputation rests above all on its research support. Exam trap: CBT does work with the client's thoughts, so 'phenomenological' tempts; but phenomenology means valuing subjective experience as it is lived, whereas CBT tests thoughts against evidence.", "topic": "CBT as evidence based", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Behavioural analysis is based upon the principles of:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Operant conditioning", "c": "Dream analysis", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: behaviour analysis is based on the principles of operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938), holds that behaviour is shaped by its consequences: reinforcement makes a behaviour more likely, while punishment and extinction make it less likely. Skinner called the laboratory study of these principles the experimental analysis of behaviour. Applied behaviour analysis (ABA) carries them into real life: Donald Baer, Montrose Wolf and Todd Risley (1968), in the first issue of the Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, defined it as applying behavioural principles to socially important behaviour and measuring the change. Its core tool is functional analysis: recording the antecedent, the behaviour and the consequence (the ABC) to find out what the behaviour gets the person or lets him escape. Treatment then changes the consequences through shaping, reinforcement schedules, extinction and token economies. ABA is best known in the treatment of autism. Example: Kabir, aged 6, throws tantrums during homework; a functional analysis shows the tantrums get him out of work, so the plan stops letting tantrums end homework and praises him for asking politely for a break. Option b is right because behaviour analysis is Skinner's operant tradition. Exam trap: behaviour therapy as a whole also uses classical conditioning, so students choose a or 'All of the above'. Key note: behaviour therapy also uses classical conditioning (Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, 1958), but 'behaviour analysis' names Skinner's operant tradition, and 'All of the above' fails because dream analysis is psychoanalytic, so b stands.", "topic": "Behaviour analysis and operant conditioning", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "A central goal of person-centered therapy is to:", "options": {"a": "Promote stronger defense mechanisms", "b": "Help clients to live up to the ideals they may have introjected", "c": "Promote congruence between the person's experienced self and ideal self", "d": "Encourage clients to look at themselves objectively and realistically"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a central goal of person-centred therapy is to promote congruence between the person's experienced self and ideal self. Carl Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951; On Becoming a Person, 1961) held that problems arise from incongruence. A child who receives only conditional regard takes in conditions of worth, then denies or distorts experiences that do not fit the self-concept. The result is a gap between the self-concept and actual experience, and often a wide gap between the self as the person experiences it and the ideal self he feels he must become. Therapy narrows these gaps. Rogers (1957) argued that if the therapist offers the core conditions of congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard and accurate empathy, the client can safely admit denied feelings, drop the conditions of worth and move towards being a fully functioning person. His team measured change with the Q-sort (William Stephenson, 1953): clients sorted self-descriptive statements once for 'how I am' and once for 'how I would like to be'. John Butler and Gerard Haigh (1954) found that the average self and ideal-self correlation rose from about zero before client-centred therapy to about .3 after it. Example: Meera feels she should be the dutiful daughter but longs to paint; in therapy she accepts both sides, and her self and ideal self move closer. Option c is right because reducing incongruence is the textbook goal of person-centred therapy. Exam trap: option b sounds like 'living up to ideals', but introjected ideals are the conditions of worth that cause the problem. Key note: strictly Rogers spoke of congruence between the self-concept and experience, and the narrowing self and ideal-self gap was how his team measured progress; option c is the textbook phrasing, so it stands.", "topic": "Person-centred therapy goals", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "______ is a term that is used when clients believe that they have more control over potentially negative outcomes than they actually do.", "options": {"a": "Overgeneralization", "b": "Catastrophe", "c": "Reattribution of responsibility", "d": "Generating alternative interpretations"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: reattribution of responsibility is the cognitive therapy term used when clients believe they have more control over, and so more responsibility for, potentially negative outcomes than they actually do. It belongs to the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (Cognitive Therapy and the Emotional Disorders, 1976). Beck held that depression and anxiety are kept going by distorted automatic thoughts. One such distortion is personalisation: taking personal blame for events that have many causes, most of them outside one's control. Reattribution is the technique that corrects it. Therapist and client list every factor that contributed to the outcome, estimate how much each played a part, and share out responsibility realistically, sometimes by drawing a 'responsibility pie'. The aim is not to deny all responsibility, but to stop the client carrying all of it. Example: Kabir blames himself entirely because his younger brother failed his board exams, believing he should have been able to prevent it. In reattribution he lists the other factors (his brother's own effort, an illness before the exam, a hard paper) and sees that his share was small, and his guilt eases. Option c is right because the stem describes inflated control and self-blame for negative outcomes, which reattribution of responsibility is designed to address. Exam trap: overgeneralisation and catastrophising are also on Beck's list of cognitive errors, so they sound plausible; but neither concerns how much control or blame the client assigns to himself.", "topic": "Reattribution of responsibility", "fig": "Aaron Beck"}, {"q": "Fundamental difference between various psychotherapeutic approaches is:", "options": {"a": "Length of treatment", "b": "Scope of practice", "c": "Theoretical orientation", "d": "Client base"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: what fundamentally separates one psychotherapeutic approach from another is its theoretical orientation. A theoretical orientation is the set of assumptions a therapy makes about human nature, about how psychological problems develop and about how change happens. Those assumptions decide what the therapist does. Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (from the 1890s) sees problems as unconscious childhood conflict, so the analyst interprets and builds insight. Behaviour therapy sees problems as learned habits, so the therapist arranges new learning through exposure or reinforcement. Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (from the 1960s) sees faulty thinking, so client and therapist test thoughts against evidence. Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy (1951) sees blocked growth, so the therapist offers empathy and acceptance. Example: Meera has exam anxiety. A psychodynamic therapist explores her fear of disappointing her father; a behaviour therapist exposes her step by step to mock tests; a cognitive therapist challenges her belief \"one bad mark means I am a failure\". Same client, same problem, different theory, so a different therapy. Option c is right because theory is the defining feature of an approach and its techniques follow from it; length, scope and client base vary inside every approach. Exam trap: the common factors view says bona fide therapies give roughly equal results through shared ingredients such as the alliance, empathy and hope. Saul Rosenzweig (1936) first argued this, quoting the Dodo in Alice in Wonderland (\"everybody has won, and all must have prizes\"), and Bruce Wampold (The Great Psychotherapy Debate, 2001) developed it. That is a claim about outcomes, not about what defines an approach: the approaches still differ in theory even when their results are similar.", "topic": "Theoretical orientation in therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Things such as socio-cultural values, political movements, historical events, economic features are known as ______ within which counselling theories originate.", "options": {"a": "Factors", "b": "Context", "c": "Boundaries", "d": "Schools"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: socio-cultural values, political movements, historical events and economic conditions make up the context within which counselling theories originate. No theory is born in a vacuum; each reflects the time, place and people that produced it. Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900) built psychoanalysis while treating middle-class patients in Vienna around 1900, a society that kept open talk of sex under tight control, and his theory put repressed sexual wishes at its centre. Carl Rogers (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942) developed his non-directive, later client-centred, approach in an individualistic, democratic America that prized self-direction. Feminist therapy grew out of the women's movement of the 1960s and 1970s, and multicultural counselling out of the civil rights era. John Sommers-Flanagan and Rita Sommers-Flanagan made this the organising theme of their textbook Counseling and Psychotherapy Theories in Context and Practice (2004). Example: Arjun, from a joint family in Lucknow, makes career decisions together with his parents. A counsellor who knows that most Western theories grew in individualistic cultures will not rush to call this dependence or enmeshment. Option b is right because \"context\" is the term for this surrounding web of social, political, historical and economic forces. Exam trap: \"Factors\" sounds plausible, because these are indeed influencing factors, but the word used for the setting in which a theory arises is context; and \"schools\" are the theories themselves, not the background they grow out of.", "topic": "Context of counselling theories", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Every psychotherapeutic approach has two components:", "options": {"a": "Theory and practice", "b": "Case conceptualization and practice", "c": "Theory and case conceptualization", "d": "Practice and evaluation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: every psychotherapeutic approach has two components, a theory and a practice. The theory is the explanation: how personality develops, how problems arise and what must change for the person to get better. The practice is what the therapist actually does: the techniques, the kind of relationship offered and the structure of sessions, all of which follow from the theory. Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (begun in 1955 as rational therapy) holds in theory that irrational beliefs, not events, cause emotional upset (the ABC model); in practice the therapist disputes those beliefs and sets homework. Joseph Wolpe's (1958) theory that anxiety is a conditioned response leads in practice to systematic desensitisation. Between the two sits case conceptualisation (case formulation): applying the theory to one client to explain why this person has these problems now and to plan treatment. Evaluation then checks whether the practice worked. Example: Kabir's counsellor follows Aaron Beck's cognitive theory (from the 1960s). She conceptualises his low mood as driven by the belief \"nobody likes me\", then in practice uses thought records and behavioural experiments to test it. Option a is right because theory and practice are the two pillars on which every approach stands; the pairing is also the theme of John Sommers-Flanagan and Rita Sommers-Flanagan's textbook Counseling and Psychotherapy Theories in Context and Practice (2004). Exam trap: case conceptualisation and evaluation are real clinical terms, so options that pair them with theory or practice look sophisticated, but each one swaps a sub-step for one of the two basic components.", "topic": "Theory and practice in therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following are the only therapeutic approaches that existed in the first half of the 20th century?", "options": {"a": "Jungian and Gestalt", "b": "Adlerian and Psychoanalytic", "c": "Jungian and Person-Centered", "d": "Person-Centered and Adlerian"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the first half of the 20th century the established systems of therapy were Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and Alfred Adler's individual psychology (Adlerian therapy). Freud developed psychoanalysis in Vienna from the 1890s (Studies on Hysteria with Josef Breuer, 1895; The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900); free association, dream interpretation and analysis of transference uncover repressed childhood conflict. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), once in Freud's circle, broke away in 1911 and founded individual psychology. He put feelings of inferiority, striving for superiority, social interest and the style of life in place of sexual drives, and made therapy an equal, educational partnership that explores birth order and early memories. The other approaches in the options came later. Carl Rogers set out non-directive counselling in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942); it became client-centred in 1951 and person-centred in the 1970s. Gestalt therapy was launched in 1951 by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman in Gestalt Therapy. Behaviour therapy (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) and the cognitive therapies (Albert Ellis, 1955; Aaron Beck, 1960s) belong to the second half. Example: Riya draws a timeline for revision; psychoanalysis and Adlerian therapy go in the pre-1950 column, and the person-centred, Gestalt, behavioural and cognitive therapies go in the 1940s to 1960s columns. Option b is right because it is the only pair in which both approaches were already full therapy systems in that period. Exam trap: every wrong option contains Gestalt or person-centred therapy, which are mid-century, and Carl Jung (who broke with Freud in 1913) tempts because he was Freud's contemporary. Key note: Jung's analytical psychology dates from 1913 and Rogers's non-directive counselling from 1942, so the word \"only\" is too strong and option c has some case; but psychoanalysis and Adlerian therapy are the pair counselling texts present as the established approaches of that period, so b stands.", "topic": "Early history of psychotherapy", "fig": "Sigmund Freud and Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of an untestable hypothesis?", "options": {"a": "Appeal to unscientific notions", "b": "Inadequate definition of concepts", "c": "Circularity", "d": "Non-directionality"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: non-directionality is not a characteristic of an untestable hypothesis, because a non-directional hypothesis is perfectly testable. A hypothesis is testable when some possible observation could show it to be wrong; Karl Popper (1934) made this falsifiability the mark of a scientific claim. John Shaughnessy, Eugene Zechmeister and Jeanne Zechmeister, in their textbook Research Methods in Psychology, list three marks of an untestable hypothesis. First, the concepts are not adequately defined: \"stress harms memory\" cannot be tested until stress and memory have operational definitions (stress as a cortisol level, memory as words recalled from a list). Second, circularity: the event is used as its own explanation. \"Kabir is lazy because he does not work, and we know he is lazy because he does not work\" can never be disproved. Third, an appeal to ideas or forces not recognised by science, such as ghosts, fate or bad luck, which science has no way to measure. A non-directional (two-tailed) hypothesis predicts that a difference or relationship exists without saying which way: \"boys and girls will differ in reading speed\". A directional (one-tailed) hypothesis names the direction: \"girls will read faster\". Both can be tested; the non-directional one simply uses both tails of the distribution. Option d is right because direction concerns how a hypothesis is worded, not whether it can be tested. Exam trap: students link \"non-directional\" with \"vague\" and so with \"untestable\", but a hypothesis is untestable only when its terms are undefined, its logic is circular or it relies on forces outside science, not when it leaves out the direction of the effect.", "topic": "Untestable hypotheses", "fig": ""}, {"q": "One of the preoccupations of quantitative researchers is with generalization which is an indicator of:", "options": {"a": "External validity", "b": "External reliability", "c": "Internal validity", "d": "Internal reliability"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: generalisation, one of the main preoccupations of quantitative researchers, is an indicator of external validity. Alan Bryman (Social Research Methods, 2001) lists four preoccupations of quantitative research: measurement (are concepts measured reliably and validly?), causality (does X really cause Y?), generalisation (do the findings apply beyond the people and setting studied?) and replication (could another researcher repeat the study and get the same result?). External validity is the extent to which results generalise to other people (population validity), other settings (ecological validity) and other times. Internal validity is the confidence that the independent variable, and not something else, caused the change in the dependent variable. Donald Campbell (1957) first set internal against external validity, and with Julian Stanley (1963) listed the threats to each. Quantitative researchers pursue generalisation mainly through random sampling from a defined population, which lets findings be extended to that population with known error. Example: Meera surveys 200 randomly chosen college students in Pune about exam stress. If her result holds for college students across Maharashtra, her study has good external validity; if it is true only of her own college, it does not. Option a is right because \"can the findings be generalised?\" is exactly the question external validity asks. Exam trap: the options cross two words (internal, external) with two others (validity, reliability). Drawing on Margaret LeCompte and Judith Goetz (1982), Bryman uses external reliability for whether a study can be replicated; internal reliability means the items of a scale (or, in qualitative work, the observers) agree. So replication goes with reliability, causality with internal validity and generalisation with external validity.", "topic": "Generalisation and external validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Measurement scale which allows researchers and statisticians to perform certain operations on data collected from respondents is classified as:", "options": {"a": "Interval scale", "b": "Flow measuring scale", "c": "Validity scale", "d": "Reliability scale"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the interval scale is the measurement scale that lets researchers and statisticians perform arithmetic operations such as addition and subtraction on the data they collect. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding a property. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, religion, a jersey number). An ordinal scale ranks them (class rank, socio-economic class), but the gaps between ranks are unknown. An interval scale adds equal units: the rise from 20 to 30 degrees Celsius is the same as from 30 to 40, so adding, subtracting and averaging make sense, and means, standard deviations and Pearson's r can be computed. Its zero is arbitrary, a reference point and not the absence of the quantity, so ratios are meaningless: 40 degrees Celsius is not twice as hot as 20. A ratio scale adds a true zero (height, weight, reaction time, income), so multiplication and division work as well. Example: Arjun scores 110 and Riya 100 on an IQ test treated as interval data; Arjun is 10 points higher, but he is not 10 percent more intelligent. Option a is right because interval is the only true level of measurement among the options and the first level at which arithmetic on the numbers is meaningful. Exam trap: \"validity scale\" and \"reliability scale\" sound like measurement scales, but validity and reliability are properties of tests; the MMPI's validity scales (L, F and K) detect test-taking attitudes. Key note: the ratio scale allows even more operations (multiplication, division, ratios) and the stem is vague, but interval is the only level of measurement among the options (flow-measuring scales are engineering instruments), so a stands.", "topic": "Interval scale of measurement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a threat to the internal validity of an experiment?", "options": {"a": "Demand characteristics", "b": "Within-groups design", "c": "Experimenter effects", "d": "Confounding variables"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a within-groups design is not a threat to internal validity; it is a research design. Internal validity is the confidence that the independent variable (IV), and nothing else, caused the change in the dependent variable (DV), and a threat is any rival explanation. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed eight classic threats: history, maturation, testing, instrumentation, statistical regression, selection, experimental mortality (drop-out) and selection-maturation interaction. The other options are threats too. A confounding variable changes along with the IV, so its effect cannot be separated from the IV's. Demand characteristics, named by Martin Orne (1962), are cues that tell participants what the experimenter expects, so they act out the hypothesis. Experimenter effects are the researcher's expectations shaping the results: Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode (1963) told students their rats were \"maze-bright\" or \"maze-dull\", and the \"bright\" rats, though labelled at random, learned faster. A within-groups (repeated-measures) design tests the same people in every condition. That removes individual differences as a confound, which strengthens internal validity. It brings order effects (practice, fatigue, carryover), but these are handled by counterbalancing, varying the order of conditions across participants. Example: Kabir tests whether music helps memory; every participant learns one list in silence and one with music, half doing silence first and half music first. Option b is right because a design is a way of controlling variables, not a rival explanation for the results. Exam trap: order effects tempt students to call the whole design a threat; the threat is an uncontrolled order effect, not the design itself.", "topic": "Threats to internal validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is an accurate criticism of projective tests of personality?", "options": {"a": "They have poor interjudge reliability, but they show high validity", "b": "They require too many inferences on the part of the examiner and do not show high validity", "c": "Their reliability is too high", "d": "They prevent subject from expressing themselves"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: an accurate criticism of projective tests is that they require too many inferences on the part of the examiner and do not show high validity. A projective test presents an ambiguous stimulus, such as an inkblot (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), a vague picture (the Thematic Apperception Test of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) or an unfinished sentence, and assumes the person projects needs and conflicts into the answer. Because answers are free and open, the examiner must infer what each response means, and different examiners can read the same record differently. The main criticisms follow from this. Interscorer reliability is often weak unless a structured system such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) is used; test-retest reliability is low, because stories change from one occasion to the next; and validity for most purposes, including diagnosis, is poor, with little gain over an interview and a questionnaire. Scott Lilienfeld, James Wood and Howard Garb (2000) reviewed the evidence and found support for only a few indices, such as Rorschach measures of thought disorder. Loren Chapman and Jean Chapman (1967, 1969) showed how clinicians come to trust invalid signs through illusory correlation. Example: two psychologists score Riya's TAT stories; one sees hidden aggression, the other sees achievement striving. That disagreement is the inference problem. Option b is right because it names both core problems: heavy examiner inference and weak validity. Exam trap: option a is half right, since poor interjudge reliability is a real criticism, but it adds \"high validity\"; reliability limits validity, so a test that scorers cannot agree on cannot be highly valid.", "topic": "Criticisms of projective tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Rorschach Inkblot Test was standardised on which method?", "options": {"a": "Exner's method", "b": "Murray's System", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Rorschach Inkblot Test was standardised by John Exner's method, the Comprehensive System (1974). Hermann Rorschach (Psychodiagnostik, 1921) published ten inkblots but died in 1922 without leaving a full scoring system. In the United States five rival systems grew up: those of Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski, and David Rapaport with Roy Schafer. Each coded responses differently, so research findings could not be compared. John Exner (1928 to 2006) studied all five, kept the elements with the best research support and combined them into one standardised procedure for administration, scoring and interpretation. His Structural Summary turns the codes (location, determinants such as form, colour and movement, form quality, content) into ratios and indices compared against norms, such as the Depression Index and the Suicide Constellation. A valid record needs at least 14 responses. After his death Gregory Meyer and colleagues published a successor, the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011). Example: Arjun's Rorschach is scored in a hospital in Bengaluru and again in one in Delhi; because both follow Exner's rules and norms, the two results can be compared. Option a is right because Exner's Comprehensive System is the method that standardised the Rorschach. Exam trap: Henry Murray is the name tied to a different projective test, the Thematic Apperception Test, which he created with Christiana Morgan (1935) and scored with his need-press system; it has nothing to do with the Rorschach.", "topic": "Exner's Comprehensive System", "fig": "John Exner"}, {"q": "What is Rorschach Inkblot Test designed to measure?", "options": {"a": "Dreams", "b": "Conscious desires", "c": "Unconscious intentions", "d": "Brain sizes"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Rorschach Inkblot Test is designed to measure unconscious motives and intentions, the hidden needs, conflicts and ways of seeing that a person cannot easily report. Hermann Rorschach, a Swiss psychiatrist, published it in Psychodiagnostik (1921). It has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, always shown in the same order: five achromatic (black and grey), two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be, and the examiner then goes back through the cards in an inquiry to learn where and why each percept was seen. The test rests on the projective hypothesis: when a stimulus is ambiguous and has no right answer, the structure a person puts into it must come from inside the person. Lawrence Frank (1939) gave these methods the name projective. So a response is taken to reveal personality structure, conflicts and unconscious processes that the person is not aware of and could not report on a questionnaire. Responses are scored for location (whole blot or detail), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), form quality and content. Example: Kabir sees \"two people pulling something apart\" in several cards; the examiner treats a repeated theme like this as a clue to his inner conflicts, not as a statement he chose to make. Option c is right because uncovering what lies outside awareness is the stated purpose of the test. Exam trap: conscious desires are what self-report inventories such as the MMPI or the 16PF tap; the Rorschach was built because people cannot or will not report what is unconscious.", "topic": "Purpose of the Rorschach", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Psychologists working with hospitalized mental patients may find ______ helpful to record the frequency of patients' aggression, self-care, speech and unusual behaviour.", "options": {"a": "Behavioural Assessment", "b": "Personality Questionnaire", "c": "Rating scale", "d": "Observation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: psychologists working with hospitalised mental patients may find behavioural assessment helpful for recording how often patients show aggression, self-care, speech and unusual behaviour. Behavioural assessment grew with behaviour therapy in the 1960s and 1970s. Instead of inferring hidden traits, it measures specific target behaviours directly, where they happen, in terms of frequency, duration and intensity, usually before and during treatment. Each behaviour first gets an operational definition (for example, aggression means hitting, kicking or throwing objects at a person). Staff then record it by event recording (counting each occurrence) or by interval recording and time sampling (checking at set moments whether it is occurring), often on a ward checklist. Functional analysis, rooted in the operant conditioning of B. F. Skinner (1938), adds what comes before and after each behaviour: antecedent, behaviour, consequence (the ABC). A classic ward example is Teodoro Ayllon and Jack Michael (1959), \"The psychiatric nurse as a behavioral engineer\": nurses recorded the frequency of patients' problem behaviours and then changed their consequences. The token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) rested on the same daily counts. Example: on a ward in Ranchi, nurses tally each time Sunil hits someone and each time he dresses himself; the counts show whether his treatment plan is working. Option a is right because counting how often specific behaviours occur is the defining method of behavioural assessment. Exam trap: rating scale and observation look right because they are real tools used on wards, but they are techniques inside behavioural assessment, not the approach the stem describes. Key note: rating scales and direct observation are the tools used inside behavioural assessment (a ward behaviour rating scale would also do the job), but the approach built around recording how often specific behaviours occur is behavioural assessment, so a stands.", "topic": "Behavioural assessment on wards", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Non-sampling error is reduced by:", "options": {"a": "Increasing sample size", "b": "Decreasing sample size", "c": "Reducing amount of data", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: non-sampling error is reduced by decreasing the sample size, in the sense that a smaller, well-controlled survey leaves fewer chances for such errors than a large survey or a complete census. Survey error has two parts. Sampling error is the chance gap between a sample statistic and the population parameter, caused only by which cases happen to be drawn. It shrinks as the sample grows, because the standard error equals the standard deviation divided by the square root of n, and it is zero in a census. Non-sampling errors are everything else: measurement and recording mistakes, badly worded questions, non-response, incomplete coverage of the population, interviewer bias, and coding and data-entry errors. They occur in a census too, and they tend to grow as the operation grows, because more respondents, more interviewers and more data handling mean more chances for mistakes and looser supervision. This is why statistics texts say a carefully run sample can be more accurate than a census. Example: Arjun's team must survey a district of 50,000 homes. Visiting every home needs hundreds of hastily trained enumerators and produces missed homes and coding slips; a sample of 2,000 homes covered by a few well-trained, closely supervised interviewers has some sampling error but far less non-sampling error. Option b is right because non-sampling error rises with the size of the survey, so a smaller, better-managed sample reduces it. Exam trap: option a is the remedy for sampling error; increasing n reduces sampling error but tends to increase non-sampling error. Key note: the real remedies for non-sampling error are careful design, pre-testing, trained field staff, follow-up of non-respondents and data checking, so \"None of these\" has a case; but the textbook point this item tests is that non-sampling error rises with the size of the survey, so b stands.", "topic": "Non-sampling error", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The difference between statistic and parameter is:", "options": {"a": "Random error", "b": "Sampling error", "c": "Standard error", "d": "Error"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the difference between a statistic and a parameter is sampling error. A parameter describes a whole population (the population mean, mu); a statistic describes a sample drawn from it (the sample mean, x-bar) and is used to estimate the parameter. Because a sample contains only some of the population, chance decides which cases are in it, so the statistic almost never equals the parameter exactly. That chance gap, statistic minus parameter, is sampling error. The American educational statistician Everet Franklin Lindquist defined it as the difference between a parameter and an estimate of that parameter derived from a sample. It implies no mistake by the researcher; it shrinks as the sample grows and is zero in a full census. If many samples are drawn, their statistics form a sampling distribution, and the standard deviation of that distribution is the standard error: the typical size of sampling error (for the mean, the standard deviation divided by the square root of n). Example: the true mean IQ of all 5,000 students in a Pune college is 102. Meera's random sample of 50 students has a mean of 99. The 3-point gap is the sampling error of her sample; another sample might miss by 1 or by 4. Option b is right because sampling error is defined as exactly this difference between a sample value and the population value. Exam trap: standard error is the closest distractor, but it is the standard deviation of such errors across repeated samples, a measure of their typical size, not the difference for one sample.", "topic": "Sampling error", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Concerning \"authorship\" in educational research, intellectual ownership is predominantly a function of:", "options": {"a": "Effort expended", "b": "Creative contribution", "c": "Professional position", "d": "Level of higher education"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: in research, intellectual ownership, and so authorship, is mainly a function of creative contribution. Authorship belongs to the people who made a substantial scientific or professional contribution to the work: forming the idea or hypothesis, designing the study, analysing and interpreting the data, and writing the paper. The Ethics Code of the American Psychological Association (2002), Standard 8.12 on publication credit, says authorship should reflect the relative scientific or professional contributions of those involved, regardless of their status, and that holding an institutional position such as department head does not by itself justify authorship. Minor contributions, such as data entry, typing, routine help with recruitment or running a standard analysis, are recognised in an acknowledgement, not by authorship. The order of authors should follow the size of each person's contribution, and a student is usually listed first on a multi-author article based mainly on the student's own dissertation (Standard 8.12c). Adding a senior person who did nothing is gift (honorary) authorship and is unethical. Example: Riya thought of the study, designed it and wrote the paper; her supervisor helped plan the analysis; a lab assistant entered data for months. Riya is first author, the supervisor second, and the assistant is thanked in the acknowledgements, however many hours she put in. Option b is right because authorship follows intellectual and creative input, not effort, rank or degrees. Exam trap: effort expended feels fair, but long hours of routine work earn an acknowledgement, not authorship, while a short but decisive creative contribution can earn it.", "topic": "Authorship and publication credit", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Identify the term that refers to a post-study interview in which all aspects of the study are revealed, reasons for the use of deception are given, and the participants' questions are answered:", "options": {"a": "Desensitizing", "b": "Debriefing", "c": "Dehoaxing", "d": "Deploying"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the post-study interview in which all aspects of the study are revealed, the reasons for any deception are explained and participants' questions are answered is called debriefing. Some studies cannot reveal their true purpose in advance, because knowing it would change how participants behave. The Ethics Code of the American Psychological Association (Standard 8.08) therefore requires researchers to give participants a prompt chance to learn the nature, results and conclusions of the research, to correct any misconceptions, and to take steps to reduce any harm the procedure caused. David Holmes (1976) split debriefing into two parts. Dehoaxing convinces participants that the false information they were given was false. Desensitising helps them deal with any distress or unwelcome self-knowledge the study produced. In the obedience studies of Stanley Milgram (1963), participants afterwards met the \"learner\", unharmed, and were later sent a written report on the study (dehoaxing), and they were reassured that their behaviour was normal and shared by many others (desensitising). Example: Riya tells participants that a test measures intelligence when it really measures reactions to failure. Afterwards she explains the real aim, why she had to mislead them and that the failure feedback was fake, and answers their questions. That whole conversation is debriefing. Option b is right because debriefing is the name for the complete post-study interview the stem describes. Exam trap: dehoaxing and desensitising are real terms, but each is only one part of debriefing, while the stem describes the whole interview. Key note: dehoaxing and desensitising are both parts of debriefing, so the whole post-study interview described is debriefing, and b stands.", "topic": "Debriefing after deception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following values could not represent a correlation coefficient?", "options": {"a": "r = 1.09", "b": "r = 0.99", "c": "r = -0.73", "d": "r = 1.0"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: r = 1.09 could not be a correlation coefficient, because a correlation coefficient can only take values from minus 1 to plus 1. Karl Pearson (1896) gave the product-moment correlation coefficient, r, its formula: the covariance of two variables divided by the product of their standard deviations. Dividing by the standard deviations puts r on a fixed scale on which no relationship can be stronger than perfect. The sign shows direction: positive when both variables rise together, negative when one rises as the other falls. The size, ignoring the sign, shows strength: 0 means no linear relationship and 1 means a perfect one. So r = 1.0 is possible: a perfect positive correlation, with every point on a rising straight line (temperature in Celsius against the same temperature in Fahrenheit, for example). r = 0.99 is a very strong positive correlation, and r = minus 0.73 is a strong negative one, exactly as strong as plus 0.73 but in the opposite direction. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination, so r = 0.99 means about 98 percent shared variance. Example: Kabir correlates study hours with marks and gets 1.09. He does not report a \"better than perfect\" relationship; he knows he has made a calculation error and checks his sums. Option a is right because 1.09 lies outside the possible range of r. Exam trap: students sometimes reject r = 1.0 as too perfect to be real; it is rare with real data, but it is a possible value, whereas anything above plus 1 or below minus 1 is impossible.", "topic": "Range of the correlation coefficient", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A related samples t-test:", "options": {"a": "Tests the null hypothesis that, in the population, the two medians are equal", "b": "Is applicable to contingency table", "c": "Is appropriate for the data from a within subjects experiment", "d": "Is a non-parametric test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a related samples t-test is appropriate for the data from a within-subjects experiment. The related t-test (also called the paired, dependent or repeated-measures t-test) compares two means that come from the same participants measured twice, in two conditions or before and after a treatment, or from matched pairs. It works on difference scores: for each person it subtracts one score from the other, then tests the null hypothesis that in the population the mean difference is zero. Because each person serves as their own control, individual differences drop out of the error term, so the test is more powerful than an independent t-test on the same number of scores. Degrees of freedom equal the number of pairs minus 1. It is a parametric test: it assumes interval or ratio data and roughly normal difference scores. William Sealy Gosset published the t distribution in 1908 under the pen name \"Student\", because his employer, the Guinness brewery, did not let staff publish under their own names. Example: Meera measures the anxiety of 20 students before and after a yoga programme. The same people give both scores, so she runs a related t-test on the 20 differences, with 19 degrees of freedom. Option c is right because a within-subjects design produces exactly the paired scores this test is built for. Exam trap: option a describes a non-parametric rank test, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945), the usual alternative for related samples, which is often described as comparing medians; the t-test compares means.", "topic": "Related samples t-test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is/are the characteristics of Karl Pearson's coefficient of correlation?", "options": {"a": "Indication of degree", "b": "Indicators of the direction", "c": "A satisfactory measure", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three are characteristics of Karl Pearson's coefficient of correlation: it indicates the degree of a relationship, it indicates its direction, and it is regarded as a satisfactory measure of linear correlation. Karl Pearson (1896) gave an exact formula to the idea of co-relation described by Francis Galton (1888); the result, the product-moment coefficient r, runs from minus 1 to plus 1. Degree: the size of r, ignoring the sign, gives the strength of the relationship; 0 means no linear relationship, values near 1 mean a very strong one. Direction: the sign gives the direction; plus means both variables rise together, minus means one rises as the other falls. A satisfactory measure: because it uses every observation, rests on means and standard deviations, and has a known sampling distribution that allows significance testing and leads into regression, statistics texts call it the most satisfactory (ideal) measure of linear correlation. Its limits are that it detects only straight-line relationships, is affected by extreme values, assumes interval or ratio data and says nothing about cause. Example: Arjun finds r = minus 0.65 between hours of phone use and sleep quality among his classmates. The minus sign gives the direction (more phone use, worse sleep) and 0.65 gives the degree (fairly strong). Option d is right because degree, direction and being a satisfactory measure are all listed among the merits of Pearson's r. Exam trap: picking a or b alone, because each is true; the item tests whether you remember that r carries both direction and degree in one number and is also valued as a measure.", "topic": "Properties of Pearson's r", "fig": "Karl Pearson"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a measure of degree of association?", "options": {"a": "Probability value", "b": "Omega squared", "c": "t-value", "d": "Degrees of freedom"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: omega squared is a measure of the degree (strength) of association between the independent and the dependent variable. William Hays (1963) proposed omega squared as an estimate of the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that is accounted for by the independent variable in the population. It is an effect size, like eta squared and r squared. Eta squared, used with ANOVA, is the sum of squares for the effect divided by the total sum of squares; it describes the sample and slightly overestimates the population value, so omega squared, which corrects for this, is smaller and less biased. Effect sizes answer \"how big is the effect?\", while significance tests answer only \"is there probably an effect at all?\". Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested benchmarks for eta squared of about 0.01 small, 0.06 medium and 0.14 large. Example: Riya compares three teaching methods with 900 students and gets p = 0.004, but omega squared = 0.01. The effect is real but tiny: teaching method explains only about 1 percent of the variation in marks. Option b is right because omega squared expresses how strongly the two variables are associated, and it does not grow just because the sample is large. Exam trap: a small p value is often misread as a strong effect, but the p value is the probability of results this extreme if the null hypothesis is true, and with a large sample even a trivial association becomes highly significant.", "topic": "Omega squared and effect size", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2018": [{"q": "A group in which hypothesized cause is present is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: the group in which the hypothesised cause is present is the experimental group. In an experiment the researcher manipulates an independent variable (IV), the supposed cause or treatment, and measures its effect on a dependent variable (DV). Participants who receive the IV form the experimental group. Participants treated in exactly the same way but without the IV form the control group, which shows what would have happened anyway and serves as the baseline for comparison. Ronald Fisher (The Design of Experiments, 1935) made random assignment central to this logic: chance, not the researcher, decides who goes into which group, so the groups are equal at the start and any later difference between them can be put down to the IV. Example: Arjun wants to know whether background music improves recall. Students randomly assigned to study with music are the experimental group; those who study the same list in silence are the control group. Option b is right because the experimental group is, by definition, the one exposed to the hypothesised cause. Exam trap: examiners offer 'random group' because random assignment is part of every true experiment; but randomness describes how people are allocated to groups, not which group receives the treatment.", "traps": {"a": "The control group is the comparison group that does not receive the hypothesised cause (the IV); it is the baseline, the exact opposite of the group the stem describes.", "c": "'Random group' is not a standard research term; randomness enters through random assignment (and random sampling), which decides who goes into each group, not which group gets the treatment.", "d": "None of the above fails because the experimental group, option b, is the standard name for the group exposed to the hypothesised cause."}}, {"q": "Dr. John is interested in how environmental rewards affect helping behaviour in child. Dr. John will closely identify with ______ perspective.", "concept": "The answer is a: a researcher who studies how environmental rewards affect children's helping works within the behavioural perspective. This perspective explains behaviour by observable events in the environment: stimuli, rewards, punishments and the learning they produce. John B. Watson (1913) launched it with his manifesto 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', arguing that psychology should study only observable behaviour, not the mind. B. F. Skinner (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938) then showed how consequences shape behaviour through operant conditioning: a response followed by reinforcement becomes more likely to occur again. A behaviourist would therefore ask which rewards (praise, stickers, attention) make helping more frequent, and how they should be delivered. Example: six-year-old Arjun is praised by his teacher each time he helps a classmate pick up dropped books, and over the following weeks he helps more often; the behaviourist explains this as learning from consequences, with no need to look inside his mind. Option a is right because environmental rewards and the behaviour they strengthen are exactly the subject matter of the behavioural perspective. Exam trap: 'helping' sounds warm and humanistic, so some students pick c; but the stem's telling words are 'environmental rewards', and rewards point straight to behaviourism.", "traps": {"b": "The psychodynamic perspective (Sigmund Freud) explains behaviour by unconscious conflicts and early childhood experience, not by rewards from the environment.", "c": "The humanistic perspective (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) stresses free will, personal growth and self-actualisation; it looks at the person's inner experience rather than at external rewards.", "d": "The neuroscience (biological) perspective explains behaviour through the brain, genes, hormones and neurotransmitters, not through environmental reward and punishment."}}, {"q": "______ perspective suggests that people have little control over their lives. The ______ perspective suggests people do have control over their lives.", "concept": "The answer is c: the psychodynamic perspective suggests that people have little control over their lives, while the humanistic perspective suggests that they do have control. This is the free will versus determinism issue, one of the major issues on which introductory textbooks (for example Robert Feldman's Understanding Psychology) compare the major perspectives. Determinism holds that behaviour is caused by forces outside our conscious choice. The psychodynamic view of Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900) is strongly deterministic: by psychic determinism no act is accidental, and behaviour is driven by unconscious urges and conflicts shaped in early childhood. The humanistic perspective of Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954), the 'third force' of the 1950s and 1960s, stresses free will, personal choice and each person's innate push towards growth and self-actualisation. The other perspectives also take sides: the behavioural and neuroscience views are deterministic (environment or biology causes behaviour), and the cognitive view leans towards free will. Example: Meera keeps choosing friends who treat her badly. A psychodynamic therapist sees an unconscious repetition of a childhood pattern; a humanistic therapist believes Meera can become aware of her choices and choose differently. Option c is right because it pairs the most deterministic perspective with the one most committed to free will, in the order the blanks require. Exam trap: d names a real contrast but reverses it, putting the free-will perspective in the 'little control' blank.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive/Neuroscience runs the wrong way: the cognitive perspective, which studies thinking and information processing, leans towards free will, and the neuroscience perspective is deterministic.", "b": "Neuroscience/Behavioural pairs two deterministic perspectives (biology or environment controls behaviour), so neither fits the second blank, which needs a perspective that stresses control over one's life.", "d": "Humanistic/Behavioural reverses the contrast: humanistic psychology stresses free will, so it cannot fill the 'little control' blank, and behaviourism (B. F. Skinner argued in Beyond Freedom and Dignity, 1971, that free will is an illusion) cannot fill the 'do have control' blank."}}, {"q": "In comparison to experiments done in psychological laboratories, experiments done in natural settings are likely to have:", "concept": "The answer is b: experiments done in natural settings (field experiments) are likely to have more problems in exerting experimental control than laboratory experiments. In a laboratory experiment the researcher creates exactly the conditions wanted, holds extraneous variables constant, assigns participants at random and measures the dependent variable precisely; this gives high internal validity, that is, confidence that the IV caused the change. A field experiment still manipulates an IV, but in a real setting such as a train, a school or a street. There the researcher cannot control noise, crowds, who happens to pass by or what else is going on; random assignment is often hard, and measurement is rougher. In exchange, field experiments have higher ecological (external) validity, and participants often do not know they are being studied, so demand characteristics are fewer. A classic example is the study by Irving Piliavin, Judith Rodin and Jane Piliavin (1969), who staged a collapse on New York subway trains to see who would help. Example: Kabir tests whether a polite sign reduces littering by placing it in some parks and not others; weather, crowds and park staff all vary in ways he cannot control. Option b is right because loss of control is the main cost of moving an experiment out of the laboratory. Exam trap: a reverses the trade-off; the laboratory, not the field, gives easier control. Key note: options a and b both end with a stray phrase, 'about the same number of problems', probably a leftover second clause from the source test bank; read the options on their first half, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Fewer problems of control is the reverse of the truth: tight control is the laboratory's strength, and natural settings bring in extraneous variables the researcher cannot hold constant.", "c": "No problems is never true of any research setting; field experiments trade control for realism, just as laboratory experiments trade realism for control.", "d": "None of the above fails because option b correctly states the main weakness of field experiments, reduced experimental control."}}, {"q": "According to Skinnerian Operant Conditioning theory, a negative reinforcement is:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the older teaching-aptitude wording this item copies, negative reinforcement is described as the withdrawal or removal of a positive reinforcer. To understand the term properly, start with operant conditioning as B. F. Skinner (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938; Science and Human Behavior, 1953) set it out and as textbooks now tabulate it: every consequence is classified by asking two questions. First, does the consequence make the behaviour more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. That gives four cells. Positive reinforcement: something pleasant, a positive reinforcer, is added (praise for finished homework), so the behaviour increases. Negative reinforcement: something aversive is removed (or avoided), so the behaviour increases; this covers escape and avoidance learning. Example: Riya takes a painkiller and her headache stops, so she reaches for a painkiller again the next time. Positive punishment: something aversive is added (a scolding), so the behaviour decreases. Negative punishment: something pleasant is removed (time-out, response cost, a fine), so the behaviour decreases. Option c is right because it is the definition this item is built on, and it is the only option that links 'negative' to removing something rather than to applying pain. Exam trap: the commonest confusion is to treat negative reinforcement as punishment; all reinforcement, positive or negative, increases behaviour. Key note: c is the answer this item expects, following the older teaching-aptitude wording; strictly, though, removing a positive reinforcer is negative punishment and adding an aversive stimulus (option d) is positive punishment, so no option gives the modern textbook definition of negative reinforcement (removal of an aversive stimulus); Skinner himself (1953) counted removing a positive reinforcer as punishment. The answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Nothing but punishment' is the most common confusion: negative reinforcement increases behaviour (by removing something aversive), whereas punishment, positive or negative, decreases it.", "b": "Biofeedback is a technique, not a type of reinforcement: physiological signals such as heart rate or muscle tension are fed back to the person so they can learn to control them, drawing in part on Neal Miller's 1960s work on operant control of autonomic responses.", "d": "Applying an aversive stimulus (a scolding, a smack) is positive punishment, which decreases behaviour; in negative reinforcement the aversive stimulus is taken away, not applied."}}, {"q": "What term is used to refer to the idea that the stimulation of the CR by the CS will gradually wear off over time?", "concept": "The answer is d: extinction is the gradual weakening and disappearance of a conditioned response (CR) when the conditioned stimulus (CS) is presented again and again without the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). Ivan Pavlov (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927) showed it with dogs. Once a bell (CS) had been paired with food (UCS), the bell alone made the dog salivate (CR). If the bell then sounded repeatedly with no food, salivation shrank trial by trial until it stopped. So 'wear off over time' in the stem means across CS-alone trials, not the mere passing of time; a CS that is never presented does not extinguish. Extinction is not erasure. After a rest, the CS can bring the CR back in weaker form, which Pavlov called spontaneous recovery, and this shows that extinction is new inhibitory learning laid over the old association rather than its removal. Example: Meera's phone ringtone (CS) used to come before stressful calls from her boss, so it made her tense (CR). After she changes jobs and hears the same tone many times with no bad news, the tension fades: that is extinction. Option d is right because it is the standard term for the CR weakening when the CS is no longer followed by the UCS. Exam trap: spontaneous recovery sounds like a time-based effect, but it is the return of the CR after extinction, the opposite of fading.", "traps": {"a": "Stimulus generalisation is the CR being produced by stimuli similar to the CS, as when Little Albert's conditioned fear of a white rat spread to a rabbit and a fur coat (John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner, 1920); it widens the response rather than weakening it.", "b": "'Unconditioning' is not a standard learning term; the weakening of a CR is called extinction, and deliberately replacing it with an incompatible response is counterconditioning.", "c": "Spontaneous recovery is the reappearance of an extinguished CR after a rest period; it is what happens after extinction, not the fading itself."}}, {"q": "Sensory register has all the following characteristics except:", "concept": "The answer is d: a capacity of 7 plus or minus 2 items belongs to short-term memory, not to the sensory register. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information passes from the sensory register to short-term memory and then to long-term memory. The sensory register holds a brief, literal copy of whatever reaches the senses: an exact, unprocessed image or sound. Its capacity is very large, but the trace fades within moments unless attention passes it on. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory. George Sperling (1960) revealed the size of iconic memory with his partial-report method: people saw a grid of 12 letters for 50 milliseconds and could report any one row cued by a tone just afterwards, so they must briefly have held most of the letters (about 9 of the 12); the image lasts about a quarter to half a second. Echoic memory lasts longer, about 2 to 4 seconds (Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder, 1972). Example: Kabir is not listening when his mother calls, yet a moment later he can 'replay' her last words; that is echoic memory at work. Option d is right because the limit of 7 plus or minus 2 chunks comes from George Miller's (1956) paper on short-term memory. Exam trap: the stem says 'except', so a, b and c, which are all true of the sensory register, are the distractors.", "traps": {"a": "True of the sensory register: visual (iconic) information lasts only about a quarter to half a second, as George Sperling's partial-report experiments showed.", "b": "True of the sensory register: it holds an exact, literal and unprocessed copy of the input before attention selects and encodes any of it.", "c": "True of the sensory register: auditory (echoic) information lasts longer than iconic, about 2 to 4 seconds, which is why a sentence just heard can still be replayed."}}, {"q": "Wechsler has defined intelligence as a global capacity because it characterises the individual's behaviour:", "concept": "The answer is c: David Wechsler called intelligence a global capacity because it characterises the individual's behaviour as a whole. Wechsler (The Measurement of Adult Intelligence, 1939; usually quoted from the 1944 edition) gave the most quoted definition of intelligence: 'the aggregate or global capacity of the individual to act purposefully, to think rationally and to deal effectively with his environment'. He explained both adjectives. Intelligence is global because it describes the person's behaviour as a whole, not one isolated skill. It is aggregate because it is made up of separate abilities which, though not entirely independent, can be told apart. This is why his tests give subtest scores (vocabulary, block design and so on) that combine into one full-scale IQ. He also replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), since mental age stops rising in adulthood. His scales began with the Wechsler-Bellevue (1939) and continued with the WISC (1949), the WAIS (1955) and the WPPSI (1967). Example: Meera's verbal, spatial and memory subtest scores differ, but together they describe how she copes with the world as a whole, which is her global capacity. Option c is right because 'as a whole' is Wechsler's own reason for the word 'global'. Exam trap: 'aggregate' and 'global' are easily swapped; aggregate refers to the many separate abilities, global to the whole person's behaviour.", "traps": {"a": "'Throughout the world' misreads 'global' as worldwide; Wechsler meant that intelligence applies to the whole of a person's behaviour, not that it holds across countries.", "b": "'A capacity to learn' is the learning-ability definition of intelligence offered by some early American psychologists, not Wechsler's reason for calling intelligence global.", "d": "None of the above fails because option c, 'as a whole', is exactly Wechsler's own explanation of the word global."}}, {"q": "Which of the following suggests that an emotion is produced when an event or object is perceived by the thalamus, which conveys this information simultaneously to the cerebral cortex, skeletal muscles and the autonomic nervous system?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Cannon-Bard theory holds that the thalamus relays an emotional stimulus at the same time to the cerebral cortex and to the body. Walter Cannon (1927) put it forward as an alternative to the James-Lange theory, and Philip Bard (1928) supported it with brain studies in cats. In the theory, the thalamus sends one signal upward to the cortex, which produces the conscious feeling, and another downward to the skeletal muscles and the autonomic nervous system, which produce arousal. Feeling and bodily change therefore happen together and independently; neither causes the other. Cannon's objections to James-Lange were that visceral changes are too slow to explain quick emotions, that the same changes (a racing heart, sweating) occur in very different emotions and in fever or exercise, and that animals whose viscera were cut off from the brain still showed emotion. Later research placed the main relay in the hypothalamus and limbic system rather than the thalamus, but the idea of simultaneous, independent signals is the theory's core. Example: Arjun sees a snake on the path; on this view his fear and his pounding heart arise at the same moment. Option b is right because a thalamic signal sent simultaneously to cortex and body is the defining claim of Cannon-Bard. Exam trap: c, James-Lange, is the theory Cannon attacked; it puts bodily change first and feeling second, the reverse of 'simultaneously'.", "traps": {"a": "Opponent-process theory (Richard Solomon and John Corbit, 1974) says every strong emotion triggers an opposite after-reaction that grows with repetition (a thrill followed by a let-down); it says nothing about simultaneous signals from the thalamus.", "c": "James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885) says bodily arousal comes first and the feeling is our perception of it ('we are afraid because we tremble'), so feeling follows the body rather than occurring alongside it.", "d": "Cognitive appraisal theory (Magda Arnold, 1960; Richard Lazarus, 1966) says emotion depends on how we evaluate an event's meaning for us; the decisive step is appraisal, not a thalamic relay."}}, {"q": "The desire to perform a behaviour in order to obtain an external reward such as praise, grades or money is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the desire to perform a behaviour to obtain an external reward such as praise, grades or money is extrinsic motivation. Motivation is extrinsic when the reason for acting lies outside the activity: a reward to gain or a punishment to avoid. It is intrinsic when the activity is rewarding in itself, done for interest, enjoyment or challenge. Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), in self-determination theory, place these on a continuum and argue that people thrive when three needs are met: autonomy, competence and relatedness. External rewards can backfire. Mark Lepper, David Greene and Richard Nisbett (1973) offered preschoolers who already liked drawing a 'Good Player' certificate for drawing; the children who expected the reward later drew less in free play than children who got no reward or an unexpected one. This is the overjustification effect: an expected external reward leads people to explain their behaviour by the reward, which undermines the interest that was already there. Example: Riya studies chemistry to win the phone her parents have promised (extrinsic), while her friend Meera studies it because she loves puzzling out reactions (intrinsic). Option a is right because the stem describes acting for a reward that lies outside the task. Exam trap: d, overjustification, also involves external rewards, but it names what rewards do to intrinsic interest, not the motive itself.", "traps": {"b": "Intrinsic motivation is the opposite: doing something for its own sake, because it is interesting or enjoyable, with no external reward needed (Edward Deci and Richard Ryan).", "c": "Achievement motivation is the need to excel and meet standards of excellence (David McClelland and John Atkinson, measured with the Thematic Apperception Test); it is defined by the urge to succeed, not by seeking external rewards.", "d": "Overjustification is the effect in which an expected external reward undermines existing intrinsic interest (Mark Lepper and colleagues, 1973); it is a consequence of rewards, not the name for wanting them."}}, {"q": "The need to reduce a drive such as hunger through appropriate objects or individuals, thereby filling some crucial lack within the organism is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: a motive that reduces a drive such as hunger through appropriate objects or people, filling a crucial lack in the organism, is a deficiency (deficit) motive. Abraham Maslow (Toward a Psychology of Being, 1962) divided human motives into two kinds. Deficiency motives, or D-motives, arise from something missing: food, safety, belonging, esteem. They push the person to fill the gap, they are satisfied by appropriate things or people from outside (a meal, a protector, a friend), and once satisfied they stop motivating. Growth motives, or B-motives (from 'being'), push towards self-actualisation and the being values such as truth, beauty and wholeness; they are not used up in the same way but grow stronger as they are pursued. Maslow applied the same D and B labels to perception and to love. D-cognition is perception distorted by one's needs, seeing others mainly as means of satisfying them, whereas B-cognition sees a person or object as it is. D-love is deficiency love, needing another to fill one's own gap, whereas B-love is unselfish love of the other's being. Example: hungry after a long exam, Kabir can think of nothing except lunch; once he eats, the urge disappears, a classic deficiency motive. Option a is right because filling a lack through outside objects or people is Maslow's definition of deficiency motivation; 'deficit motive' is a variant wording of the same term. Exam trap: c and d also begin with 'D', for deficiency, but they name kinds of perception and love, not kinds of motive.", "traps": {"b": "Growth (being) motives are Maslow's higher motives towards self-actualisation; they do not fill a lack and are not satisfied by outside objects, so they are the opposite of what the stem describes.", "c": "D-cognition is Maslow's term for need-driven perception, seeing people and things through the lens of one's own deficiencies (as opposed to B-cognition); it is a way of perceiving, not a motive.", "d": "D-love is Maslow's deficiency love, love that needs the other person to fill one's own gap (as opposed to unselfish B-love); it is a type of love, not the general motive described."}}, {"q": "______ is a motivated state caused by physiological deprivation such as lack of food or water.", "concept": "The answer is c: the motivated state caused by physiological deprivation is a drive, which Clark Hull (1943) wrote as D. In Hull's drive-reduction theory (Principles of Behavior, 1943), a need is the physiological deprivation itself: the body's tissues lack water or food. A drive is the aroused, tense, energising state that the need produces, and it pushes the animal to act. Any response that reduces the drive is reinforced and so learned. Hull wrote drive as D in his equation for reaction potential, in which habit strength is multiplied by drive: a well-learned habit is performed only when a drive is present to energise it. Drive reduction restores homeostasis, the body's tendency to keep a stable internal balance, a term Walter Cannon coined and set out in The Wisdom of the Body (1932). Example: after a day of fasting, Arjun's body lacks food (the need); he feels restless and driven to find something to eat (the drive); eating reduces the drive, and the route he took to the canteen is reinforced. Option c is right because the stem describes the motivated state, not the deprivation, and that state is Hull's drive, D. Exam trap: a, need, tempts because the stem mentions deprivation; but the need is the lack itself, and the motivated state it causes is the drive. Key note: some introductory texts use 'need' loosely for this state, and 'D-Drive' is an unusual label (read it as Hull's drive, D); but a need is the deprivation itself and the motivated state it causes is a drive, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A need is the physiological deprivation itself (the body's lack of food or water); it causes the motivated state but is not that state, which Hull called drive.", "b": "An instinct is an innate, unlearned, species-typical pattern of behaviour (William McDougall, 1908, listed human instincts); drive theory replaced instinct theory, and an instinct is not a state caused by deprivation.", "d": "Homeostasis is the body's tendency to keep a stable internal balance (Walter Cannon); deprivation upsets homeostasis and drive reduction restores it, so homeostasis is the balance being defended, not the motivated state."}}, {"q": "The unconscious perception of stimuli that are too weak to exceed the absolute threshold for detection is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: subliminal perception is the unconscious perception of stimuli too weak or brief to exceed the absolute threshold for detection. 'Limen' is Latin for threshold, so subliminal means below the threshold. The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus a person detects 50 per cent of the time (Gustav Fechner, 1860). A subliminal stimulus is not consciously reported, yet it can still be processed. The best evidence comes from masked priming: Anthony Marcel (1983) flashed words so briefly, followed by a pattern mask, that people could not say what they had seen, yet an unseen word still speeded their responses to a related word. Such effects are real but small and short-lived. The famous claim of James Vicary (1957) that flashing 'Eat popcorn' and 'Drink Coca-Cola' during a film raised cinema sales was a hoax that he admitted in 1962, and there is no good evidence that subliminal advertising changes what people buy. Example: Riya sees a smiling face for a few milliseconds, instantly masked; she reports seeing nothing, but then rates an unfamiliar Chinese character a little more favourably. Option c is right because the stem defines subliminal perception exactly. Exam trap: a and b are one and the same thing, the just noticeable difference or difference threshold (Ernst Weber, 1834), which concerns noticing a change between two stimuli, not perceiving a stimulus below the absolute threshold.", "traps": {"a": "The just noticeable difference is the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect half the time (Ernst Weber's law); it concerns differences between stimuli, not stimuli below the absolute threshold.", "b": "The difference threshold is simply another name for the just noticeable difference, so it fails for the same reason: it is about detecting a change, not unconscious perception of very weak stimuli.", "d": "Response bias, from signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966), is a person's tendency to say 'yes' or 'no' whatever the signal, shaped by expectations and motives; it is not unconscious detection of weak stimuli."}}, {"q": "A motion that is produced most simply by flashing a light in darkness and then, in few milliseconds later, flashing another light near the location of the first light is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: when one light is flashed in darkness and another is flashed nearby a few milliseconds later, people see a single light moving from the first place to the second; this is stroboscopic motion, a form of apparent motion. Nothing actually moves. The brain takes two still stimuli, separated by the right interval, and fills in the path between them. The effect depends on timing: with an interval below about 30 milliseconds the two lights look simultaneous, with roughly 30 to 200 milliseconds they look like one moving light, and with longer intervals they look like two lights flashing in turn. Max Wertheimer's (1912) study of such apparent movement, the phi phenomenon, showed that the perceived motion lies not in either stimulus but in the relation between them, and it launched Gestalt psychology. Films work the same way, with 24 still frames a second seen as smooth movement, and so do the chasing lights of neon signs. Example: Meera watches the bulbs around a cinema hoarding switch on one after another and sees a ring of light racing round, though each bulb only blinks in place. Option a is right because the stem describes the standard laboratory demonstration of stroboscopic motion. Exam trap: b, real motion, is what the experience feels like, but real motion needs an object that actually moves, its image sweeping across the retina.", "traps": {"b": "Real motion is the perception of an object that is physically moving, its image sweeping across the retina and stimulating motion-detecting neurons (area MT); in the stem nothing moves at all.", "c": "Selective adaptation is the fatigue of motion-detecting neurons after long viewing of movement in one direction, which produces the motion aftereffect (the waterfall illusion); it is not produced by two brief flashes.", "d": "None of the above fails because stroboscopic motion, option a, is the exact name for the two-flash illusion the stem describes."}}, {"q": "The last stage of creative thinking is:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the classic model of creative thinking, verification is the last stage. Graham Wallas (The Art of Thought, 1926), drawing on the accounts of famous scientists and mathematicians, described four stages. Preparation: the problem is defined and studied consciously, information is gathered and solutions are tried. Incubation: the problem is set aside, and work seems to continue outside awareness. Illumination: the solution appears suddenly, the 'aha' moment of insight. Verification: the idea is tested, checked and worked out in detail to see whether it really solves the problem. The order is fixed: preparation, incubation, illumination, verification. Incubation is the stage most studied since, because it explains why a break so often helps. Example: Kabir struggles for days with a design for his science project (preparation), gives up and goes to a cricket match (incubation), suddenly sees the answer on the bus home (illumination), and then builds and tests a model to make sure it works (verification). Option a is right because testing and refining the insight is the final step in Wallas's sequence. Exam trap: b, evaluation, sounds like a natural last step, and it appears in Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's (1996) five-step account (preparation, incubation, insight, evaluation, elaboration); but there it is fourth, followed by elaboration, so it is not the last stage in either model.", "traps": {"b": "Evaluation is not a stage in Graham Wallas's model; in Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's five steps it comes fourth, before elaboration, so it is not the last stage there either.", "c": "Incubation is Wallas's second stage, the period when the problem is set aside while unconscious processing continues; it comes before illumination, not at the end.", "d": "Preparation is Wallas's first stage, when the problem is defined and information gathered; it starts the creative process rather than ending it."}}, {"q": "Proximity, continuity and closure are all forms of:", "concept": "The answer is c: proximity, continuity and closure are Gestalt laws of perceptual grouping, the principles by which we see separate elements as belonging together. The Gestalt psychologists held that the whole is different from the sum of its parts, and Max Wertheimer (1923) set out the grouping laws. Proximity: elements close together are grouped, so dots spaced in pairs look like pairs. Similarity: elements alike in colour, shape or size are grouped. Continuity, or good continuation: we follow smooth lines and curves rather than abrupt changes of direction. Closure: we fill gaps to see complete figures, so a broken circle is still seen as a circle. Common fate: elements moving together are grouped. All of them serve Prägnanz, the tendency to see the simplest, most stable organisation. Figure-ground organisation, shown by Edgar Rubin (1915) with his vase that can also be seen as two faces, is a separate and more basic process: splitting the scene into an object (the figure) and its background (the ground). Example: Riya sees a row of streetlights curving round a bend as one continuous line (continuity) and a logo made of broken arcs as a complete circle (closure). Option c is right because proximity, continuity and closure are all principles of grouping. Exam trap: d, figure-ground, is the other famous Gestalt principle of organisation, so it lures students who remember 'Gestalt' but not which process these laws belong to.", "traps": {"a": "Figures are the objects that stand out in figure-ground organisation; proximity, continuity and closure are not kinds of figure but rules for grouping elements.", "b": "Ground is the background against which a figure is seen; it is one half of figure-ground organisation, not a name for the grouping laws.", "d": "Figure-ground (Edgar Rubin, 1915) is the separate Gestalt process of separating an object from its background; the grouping laws decide which elements go together, a different question."}}, {"q": "If central tendency is found by using whole population as input data then this is classified as:", "concept": "The answer is d: a measure of central tendency computed from the whole population is a population parameter. A population is every member of the group a researcher wants to describe, such as all college students in a state; a sample is a smaller set drawn from it. A number that describes a population is a parameter; the same number calculated from a sample is a statistic. Parameters are written with Greek letters (mu for the population mean, sigma for its standard deviation), and statistics with Roman letters (x-bar or M for the sample mean, s for its standard deviation). A parameter is fixed but usually unknown, because whole populations are rarely measured; a statistic is known but varies from sample to sample, and it is used to estimate the parameter. That estimation is the work of inferential statistics. A quick memory aid: Population goes with Parameter, Sample goes with Statistic. Example: if Arjun records the age of every one of the 60 employees in his small firm and finds a mean of 34 years, that mean is a population parameter; if he measured only 15 of them, their mean would be a sample statistic. Option d is right because a value based on the whole population is by definition a parameter. Exam trap: b, 'population statistic', mixes the two words; a statistic always comes from a sample, so the phrase is not a standard term.", "traps": {"a": "A sample statistic is a value computed from a sample (x-bar, s) and used to estimate the parameter; the stem uses the whole population, so it is not a sample value.", "b": "'Population statistic' is not a standard term: statistics describe samples and parameters describe populations, and the phrase mixes the two.", "c": "'Population tendency' is a filler built from the stem's own words; the population value of a mean, median or mode is called a parameter."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is used to compare the variation or dispersion in two or more sets of data even though they are measured in different units?", "concept": "The answer is c: the coefficient of variation (CV) is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even when they are measured in different units. Karl Pearson (1896) defined it as the standard deviation expressed as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Because the SD and the mean are in the same units, the units cancel and the CV is a pure percentage. That allows comparisons the SD cannot make: heights in centimetres against weights in kilograms, or the spread of marks in a class whose mean is 30 against one whose mean is 80. The set with the lower CV is the more consistent (more uniform, less variable). Example: in Riya's class, height has a mean of 160 cm and an SD of 8 cm (CV = 5 per cent), while weight has a mean of 50 kg and an SD of 5 kg (CV = 10 per cent); weight varies relatively twice as much, even though 8 looks larger than 5. Because it divides by the mean, the CV strictly needs ratio data with a true zero. Option c is right because it is the standard relative, unit-free measure of dispersion. Exam trap: the standard deviation is the best-known measure of spread, so it attracts guesses; but it is stated in the original units, so comparing an SD in centimetres with one in kilograms is meaningless.", "traps": {"a": "Range (highest minus lowest score) is an absolute measure in the original units and depends on just two extreme scores, so it cannot compare data measured in different units.", "b": "The standard deviation is the most used measure of spread, but it is in the original units (cm, kg), so SDs of differently measured variables cannot be compared directly; dividing it by the mean gives the CV.", "d": "Mean deviation, the average absolute distance of scores from the mean, is also in the original units; its unit-free form is the coefficient of mean deviation, not mean deviation itself."}}, {"q": "For a positively skewed distribution, mean is always:", "concept": "The answer is c: in a positively skewed distribution the mean is greater than the mode. Skewness is lack of symmetry. In positive (right) skew most scores are low and a few very high scores form a long tail to the right; income in a city is the classic case. The three averages then separate. The mode stays at the peak, where most scores lie. The median, which depends only on position, moves a little towards the tail. The mean uses the value of every score, so the few large values pull it furthest into the tail. The order is therefore mean greater than median greater than mode. In negative (left) skew the order reverses. Karl Pearson (1895) noted that in moderately skewed distributions the mode is roughly 3 times the median minus 2 times the mean, and his coefficient of skewness, (mean minus mode) divided by the standard deviation, often written 3 times (mean minus median) divided by the standard deviation, is positive in right skew. Example: in Riya's town most families earn modest amounts, but a few business owners earn a great deal; the mean income is higher than the median and much higher than the mode. Option c is right because the long right tail drags the mean above the mode. Exam trap: a, 'less than the median', is true of negative skew; remember that the mean is pulled towards the tail. Key note: 'always' holds for the usual single-peaked distribution; Paul von Hippel (2005) showed exceptions in some discrete or multimodal distributions, but the textbook rule is what this item tests, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Mean less than the median describes negative (left) skew, where a few very low scores pull the mean down; in positive skew the mean is above the median.", "b": "Mean less than the mode is also the negative-skew pattern (mean, then median, then mode from left to right); in positive skew the mode is the smallest of the three.", "d": "'Difficult to tell' fails because the direction of skew fixes the order of the averages in a typical single-peaked distribution: in positive skew, mean greater than median greater than mode."}}, {"q": "If most repeated observations recorded are outliers of data then mode is considered as:", "concept": "The answer is b: if the most repeated observations are outliers, the mode is a poor measure of central tendency. The mode is simply the most frequent value in a set of data. Its job, like that of any average, is to represent the typical score. Usually the mode sits at the peak of the distribution, where most scores cluster, and it has real strengths: it is easy to find, it is not pulled about by a few extreme scores, and it is the only average for nominal data (the most common blood group, the most popular brand). But the mode looks only at frequency, not at where the value lies. If the value that repeats most often is an outlier, a value far from the bulk of the data, the mode lands away from the centre and misrepresents the typical score. The median, which depends on the middle position, would then be the better choice. Example: in a class test, 6 of Kabir's 30 classmates were absent and recorded as 0, while the other 24 scored between 50 and 80, each mark appearing only once or twice; the mode is 0, which describes almost nobody. Option b is right because a mode located at an outlier fails to describe the centre of the data. Exam trap: textbooks say the mode is unaffected by extreme values, which tempts students towards d; that is true when extremes are rare, but here the extreme values are the most frequent ones.", "traps": {"a": "'Intended measure' is not a statistical term; it is a filler with no meaning for the mode.", "c": "'Percentage measure' is a filler; percentages and percentiles are ways of expressing scores, not a description of how good the mode is.", "d": "Best measure is wrong here: the mode is the best (and only) average for nominal data, but when the most frequent values are outliers it misrepresents the centre."}}, {"q": "Psychoanalytic theory is often NOT criticised for which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is d: psychoanalytic theory is not usually criticised for being simplistic; if anything, critics call it too elaborate. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) built a large, layered system: id, ego and superego, five psychosexual stages, the Oedipus complex, a long list of defences and a code of dream symbols. The standard criticisms run in other directions. First, it is unfalsifiable: the philosopher Karl Popper (Conjectures and Refutations, 1963) argued that no possible observation could count against it. If Arjun loves his mother, that is Oedipal; if he hates her, he is defending against Oedipal love. A theory that explains every outcome predicts none. Second, it is unscientific: the data came from a small, unrepresentative group of Viennese patients, written up from memory after sessions, with no controls and no independent check. Third, it is deterministic: psychic determinism holds that every act, slip and dream is caused by unconscious forces, which leaves little room for free will. Critics also call it sexist (Karen Horney attacked the idea of penis envy in the 1920s) and say it overstresses sex and early childhood. Option d is right because 'simplistic' is the one label missing from the standard list; the theory is attacked for its complexity, not for being too simple. Exam trap: the stem asks which criticism is NOT made, so the three familiar criticisms (a, b and c) are the distractors. Key note: some critics call Freud reductionist for tracing much of behaviour back to sexual drives and early childhood, which is a kind of oversimplification; but 'simplistic' is not on the standard list of criticisms, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Unfalsifiability is the best-known criticism, made by Karl Popper (1963): no possible result could refute the theory, so it is a criticism psychoanalysis does face and cannot answer a NOT question.", "b": "Psychoanalysis is routinely called unscientific because it rests on uncontrolled case studies of a small, biased sample of patients and on the analyst's own interpretation, so this criticism is very much made.", "c": "Freud's psychic determinism, the view that unconscious forces cause every act, slip and dream, is a standard criticism (humanists such as Carl Rogers objected to it), so it is not the exception."}}, {"q": "Which Freudian defense mechanism does this statement illustrate, \"I am not snobbish, you are\"?", "concept": "The answer is a: saying 'I am not snobbish, you are' is projection, attributing one's own unacceptable trait or impulse to someone else. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) described defence mechanisms as unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety, and his daughter Anna Freud (The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, 1936) catalogued them. In projection the impulse is not removed; it is relocated. The person still notices the snobbery, anger or jealousy, but sees it in someone else, which feels far less threatening than owning it. The logic runs: 'I do not hate him; he hates me.' Example: Meera secretly envies her colleague's promotion, but tells her friends that the colleague has always been jealous of her. Projection in a broader sense also lies behind the projective hypothesis of tests such as the Rorschach: people read their own needs and conflicts into ambiguous stimuli. Option a is right because the speaker disowns a trait (snobbishness) and pins it on the listener, which is the defining move of projection. Exam trap: denial also begins with 'I am not', but denial refuses to accept a painful fact of reality ('my exam result cannot be a fail'); it does not shift a trait onto another person. A quick test: if the unwanted trait ends up located in someone else, it is projection.", "traps": {"b": "Sublimation channels an unacceptable sexual or aggressive impulse into a socially valued activity, such as anger into kickboxing; Freud saw it as the healthiest defence, but nothing here is being turned into useful work.", "c": "Repression pushes a threatening thought or memory out of awareness altogether (motivated forgetting); the speaker here is fully aware of snobbishness and simply places it in someone else.", "d": "Denial refuses to accept a painful fact of reality ('he cannot be dead'); the 'I am not' looks similar, but the statement goes further and pins the trait on another person, which is projection."}}, {"q": "What proportion of variability in most personality traits tend to be heritable?", "concept": "The answer is b: about 40 percent (roughly 40 to 50 percent) of the variation in most personality traits is heritable. Heritability is the proportion of the differences among people in a trait, within one population at one time, that can be traced to genetic differences. Behaviour genetics estimates it with two natural experiments. Twin studies compare identical (monozygotic) twins, who share all their genes, with fraternal (dizygotic) twins, who share about half; adoption studies compare children with their biological and adoptive parents. Thomas Bouchard's Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart (from 1979; main report 1990) found that identical twins raised in different homes were about as alike in personality as those raised together, putting heritability at about 0.40 to 0.50. Kerry Jang, John Livesley and Philip Vernon (1996) found roughly 40 to 60 percent for the Big Five. The surprise was that shared family environment adds almost nothing; most of the rest is non-shared environment, such as different friends, teachers and experiences (Robert Plomin and Denise Daniels, 1987). Example: Riya and her identical twin Siya, raised in different cities, are both outgoing, yet differ in their interests because of their own experiences. Option b is right because textbooks summarise the heritability of most personality traits as about 40 percent. Exam trap: 60 percent is the top of some single-trait estimates, and 80 percent mixes personality up with traits such as height or adult IQ; for most personality traits the summary figure is about 40 percent. Key note: estimates range from about 40 to 50 percent, with some studies up to 60, and introductory texts usually say 'about 40 percent' or '40 to 50 percent', so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "20 percent underestimates genetic influence; twin and adoption studies consistently put the heritability of most personality traits at about twice this figure.", "c": "60 percent is the upper end of some single-trait estimates (openness in the 1996 twin study by Kerry Jang, John Livesley and Philip Vernon), not the typical figure for most personality traits.", "d": "80 percent is closer to the heritability of height, or of adult IQ in some studies; personality traits are far less heritable, and non-shared environment explains much of the rest."}}, {"q": "What according to Rogers is the cause of all psychological problems?", "concept": "The answer is a: for Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), psychological problems arise when the actualising tendency, the innate drive to grow and fulfil one's potential, is blocked. Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951; his formal statement of the theory, 1959) held that every organism moves towards growth. As the self develops, a child needs positive regard. When love is conditional ('we love you when you top the class'), the child takes in conditions of worth and starts valuing experiences by whether they win approval, not by whether they feel right. Experiences that do not fit the self-concept are then denied or distorted. The gap between the self-concept and actual experience is incongruence, felt as tension, anxiety and confusion. Incongruence blocks the actualising tendency, and that block is what Rogers saw behind psychological disturbance. Example: Arjun was praised only for his marks and told that boys who cry are weak; as an adult he denies his sadness, feels empty and anxious, and cannot follow his love of music. Therapy removes the block by offering unconditional positive regard, empathy and genuineness, so that the growth tendency can work again. Option a is right because Rogers traced all disturbance to conditions of worth and incongruence that stop the actualising tendency. Exam trap: 'emotional disturbance' sounds like a cause but is only a description of the problem, and 'faulty learning' is the behaviourists' explanation, not the humanists'.", "traps": {"b": "Faulty learning is the behavioural account (B. F. Skinner, 1953; Joseph Wolpe, 1958): problems are maladaptive habits learned through conditioning and can be unlearned; Rogers rejected this mechanistic view.", "c": "Emotional disturbance is a description of the problem itself, not a cause; Rogers explained disturbance by the blocked actualising tendency.", "d": "Impaired awareness is closest to the Gestalt therapy of Fritz Perls (Gestalt Therapy, 1951), which aims to restore full awareness; Rogers's cause is incongruence produced by conditions of worth."}}, {"q": "Behaviour motivated by objections to the belief that society regards men as superior to women is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) called this masculine protest, a striving for power and superiority that, in women, takes the form of protest against the inferior feminine role that society assigns them. Adler first used the term in 1910 and developed it in The Neurotic Constitution (1912). His starting point was inferiority. In Study of Organ Inferiority (1907) he argued that a weak organ drives a person to compensate. He then widened the idea: every child begins small and dependent, so inferiority feelings are universal, and the effort to overcome them drives all striving. In a culture that treats men as strong and women as weak, being 'feminine' becomes a symbol of inferiority, and masculine protest is the reaction: refusing that inferior position and pushing for power and equality. Adler saw the belief in male superiority as a cultural prejudice, not a biological fact, and thought it harmed both sexes. He later folded the idea into the broader striving for superiority. Example: Meera, told that girls should not study engineering, works furiously to beat every boy in her class and refuses any task labelled 'women's work'. Option d is right because the stem describes behaviour driven by objection to the belief that men are superior, which is Adler's masculine protest. Exam trap: inferiority and superiority feelings are also Adler's terms, so every option sounds Adlerian; but they are general motives found in everyone, not the specific reaction against male superiority.", "traps": {"a": "Neglect is one of the three childhood conditions (with organ inferiority and pampering) that Adler said produce an inferiority complex and a faulty style of life; it is a cause of difficulty, not a protest against male superiority.", "b": "Superiority feelings, or the superiority complex, are Adler's term for overcompensation, an exaggerated self-importance shown in boasting or arrogance; they are not specifically about the cultural rank of men and women.", "c": "Inferiority feelings are, for Adler, universal and normal, arising from every child's helplessness; they are the starting point of all striving, not the specific reaction against male superiority."}}, {"q": "Personality psychologists such as Cattell, Spearman, Eysenck all used factor analysis in their work, as they felt this was more objective method of developing trait taxonomies. However this method is criticised for which of the following reasons?", "concept": "The answer is d: factor analysis is criticised on all three counts: the researcher chooses what goes in, decides how many factors to keep, and names them. Factor analysis is a statistical method that takes the correlations among many measures and finds a few underlying factors that explain them. Charles Spearman (1904) invented it to show a general intelligence factor, g. Raymond Cattell (1905 to 1998) applied it to trait words and ratings and arrived at 16 source traits (the 16PF, 1949); Hans Eysenck (1916 to 1997) arrived at three superfactors (extraversion, neuroticism, psychoticism); later researchers found five. The disagreement comes from three points of judgement. (1) Input: the result depends on which variables or trait terms enter the analysis; leave out a domain and no factor for it can appear (garbage in, garbage out). (2) Number of factors: how many to keep is a decision, guided by rules such as Henry Kaiser's eigenvalue-greater-than-one rule or Cattell's scree test (1966), and different rules give different answers. (3) Naming: a factor is only a cluster of loadings; calling it 'anxiety' or 'neuroticism' is the researcher's interpretation. Example: Kabir factor-analyses 40 adjectives; if he leaves out every word about honesty, no honesty factor can emerge, however important honesty is in real life. Option d is right because a, b and c are all standard criticisms of the method. Exam trap: each single option is true, so a student who spots one valid criticism may stop there; with three true statements, 'All of the above' is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "Choosing factor names is a real criticism (a factor is only a pattern of loadings and the label is the researcher's interpretation), but picking it alone misses the equally standard problems of deciding how many factors exist and choosing what to enter.", "b": "Deciding how many factors exist is a real criticism (Kaiser's rule, Cattell's scree test and other rules can give different numbers, hence 16 versus 3 versus 5), but on its own it leaves out naming and the choice of input.", "c": "Choosing the terms to enter is a real criticism (the factors can only reflect the variables put in), but on its own it leaves out the naming and number-of-factors problems, which are just as standard."}}, {"q": "When motives change to self-sustaining interests, Allport would say that they have become:", "concept": "The answer is a: Gordon Allport (1897 to 1967) said that motives which have changed into self-sustaining interests are functionally autonomous. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation (1937) Allport argued against explaining every adult motive by tracing it back to childhood drives or to past rewards. A motive may begin as a means to an end, but over time it can become independent of the need that first produced it and continue for its own sake. His example: a sailor first goes to sea to earn a living, but after he becomes rich he still craves the sea. Allport distinguished two levels. Perseverative functional autonomy covers low-level routines that keep going without reward, such as habits, rituals and addictions. Propriate functional autonomy covers higher motives tied to the self (the proprium): values, interests and life goals, which are central to mature adult motivation. Example: Riya began practising the veena only to escape her parents' scolding; now, at thirty, she plays for hours with no one asking her to. Option a is right because a motive that has turned into a self-sustaining interest, cut off from its original cause, is exactly what Allport meant by functional autonomy. Exam trap: 'habituated' sounds like 'habit', but habituation is a fall in response to a repeated stimulus; a functionally autonomous motive keeps going strongly, it does not fade.", "traps": {"b": "Extinction (Ivan Pavlov, 1927; B. F. Skinner, 1938) is the weakening and disappearance of a learned response when reinforcement stops; functionally autonomous motives do the opposite, persisting without the original reward.", "c": "Needs (as in Henry Murray's 1938 list or Abraham Maslow's hierarchy) are the deficits that start motives; Allport's point is that mature motives become independent of the needs that first produced them.", "d": "Habituation is a decline in response to a stimulus that is repeated without consequence (no longer noticing a ticking clock); it describes a fading reaction, not a motive that sustains itself."}}, {"q": "Studying groups of people in order to discover general principles concerning human behaviour:", "concept": "The answer is a: studying groups of people to discover general principles of behaviour is the nomothetic approach. The terms come from the German philosopher Wilhelm Windelband (1894), who divided the sciences into those that seek general laws (nomothetic, from Greek nomos, law) and those that study unique events (idiographic, from Greek idios, one's own). Gordon Allport (1937) brought the distinction into personality psychology. Nomothetic research measures many people on the same dimensions, compares them and looks for principles that hold for everyone: how extraversion relates to sociability, or whether anxiety rises before exams. Most trait research is nomothetic; Raymond Cattell (16PF, 1949) and Hans Eysenck (1916 to 1997) worked this way, using group statistics and factor analysis. Idiographic research studies one person in depth to grasp his or her unique pattern, as Allport did in Letters from Jenny (1965), his analysis of some 300 letters written by one woman. Example: Arjun gives a personality questionnaire to 2,000 college students to find out whether conscientiousness predicts marks; that is nomothetic. His friend Meera spends a year interviewing one artist to understand her life; that is idiographic. Option a is right because the stem describes group study aimed at general principles, which is the nomothetic approach. Exam trap: the two Greek-rooted terms are easy to swap; remember nomothetic as 'laws for all' and idiographic as 'the idiosyncratic individual'.", "traps": {"b": "The idiographic approach, favoured by Gordon Allport, studies a single individual in depth (diaries, letters, case studies) to understand what is unique; it is the opposite of studying groups for general laws.", "c": "Proprium is Allport's term for the self, the 'me' functions that give a person unity and identity; it is a concept within his theory, not a research approach.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, the nomothetic approach, is exactly the group-based search for general principles that the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Comorbidity explains the notion that:", "concept": "The answer is d: comorbidity is the notion that differently defined disorders can occur together in the same person. In psychiatry, comorbidity means the presence of two or more distinct disorders at the same time, such as major depressive disorder with generalised anxiety disorder, or alcohol use disorder with post-traumatic stress disorder. In mental health it is the rule rather than the exception. The National Comorbidity Survey of Ronald Kessler and colleagues (1994) in the United States found that more than half of all lifetime disorders occurred in the roughly one person in seven who had a history of three or more disorders. Comorbidity matters for three reasons. It complicates diagnosis, because symptoms overlap. It worsens prognosis, since comorbid cases are usually more severe and harder to treat. And it raises questions about classification: if anxiety and depression so often travel together, perhaps they share causes, which is one reason for transdiagnostic treatments. Example: Meera is diagnosed with panic disorder and, a year later, also meets the criteria for major depression; her clinician has to plan treatment for both, not just one. Option d is right because the co-occurrence of separately defined disorders in one individual is exactly what comorbidity means. Exam trap: 'morbidity' sounds like 'mortality', so examiners offer options about dying or death; comorbidity is about illnesses co-existing in a living person, and it is also different from prevalence, which is how common a disorder is in a population.", "traps": {"a": "People with the same problem dying at the same time confuses comorbidity with mortality (death rates); comorbidity is about disorders co-existing in one living person.", "b": "A chronic fear of death is thanatophobia (death anxiety), a specific fear, not the co-occurrence of disorders.", "c": "Depression in winter refers to the seasonal pattern specifier of major depression (seasonal affective disorder), and even that is not 'always' in winter; it says nothing about two disorders occurring together."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not used to define psychopathology?", "concept": "The answer is b: deviation from the sexual norm is not one of the general criteria used to define psychopathology. No single test separates normal from abnormal, so clinicians weigh several criteria together. (1) Statistical infrequency: behaviour far from the average, at the tail of the normal curve; its flaw is that rare talent, such as a genius IQ, is also deviant. (2) Deviation from social norms: behaviour that breaks a society's explicit or implicit rules; its flaw is that norms differ across cultures and eras. (3) Maladaptive behaviour, or dysfunction: behaviour that stops a person working, studying or keeping relationships. (4) Personal distress. Ronald Comer's widely used textbook summary is the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Marie Jahoda (1958) offered a positive picture of ideal mental health, and Jerome Wakefield (1992) defined disorder as harmful dysfunction. A separate 'sexual norm' is not on any of these lists, and history shows why it would be dangerous: homosexuality was treated as a disorder until the American Psychiatric Association removed it from the DSM in 1973. Example: Kabir washes his hands for three hours a day; that is unusual (statistical), distressing and stops him working (maladaptive), whatever anyone's sexual norms. Option b is right because a, c and d are standard criteria and b is not. Exam trap: 'social norm' and 'sexual norm' differ by only a few letters, and students who read quickly treat them as the same criterion.", "traps": {"a": "Statistical infrequency (deviation from the statistical norm) is a standard criterion: behaviour far from the average is labelled abnormal, although rare talent shows its limits.", "c": "Deviation from social norms is a standard criterion (the 'deviance' of the four Ds): behaviour that violates a culture's rules, limited by cultural relativity.", "d": "Maladaptive behaviour, or dysfunction, is a standard criterion and often the most useful one: behaviour that impairs work, study or relationships."}}, {"q": "Which of the following has been called the \"common cold\" of psychopathology because it occurs so frequently and because almost everyone has experienced it at some time?", "concept": "The answer is c: unipolar depression has been called the 'common cold' of psychopathology because it is so common and because nearly everyone has felt low at some point. The nickname is usually credited to Martin Seligman, writing on learned helplessness in the 1970s; it is also quoted as 'the common cold of psychiatry'. Unipolar means one pole: the person has episodes of depression only, with no mania or hypomania. In DSM-5 this is major depressive disorder: depressed mood or loss of interest plus other symptoms (changes in sleep and appetite, fatigue, worthlessness, poor concentration, thoughts of death) for at least two weeks. Bipolar disorder swings between two poles, depression and mania. The nickname fits the numbers. Lifetime prevalence of major depression is about 15 to 20 percent, and after puberty women are affected about twice as often as men. By contrast bipolar I disorder and schizophrenia each affect about 1 percent, and OCD about 2 percent. The second half of the stem matters too: ordinary sadness after loss or failure is universal, so almost everyone knows a mild version of depression, while few people know mania or psychosis from the inside. Example: Meera, after failing an entrance exam, has felt hopeless, sleepless and uninterested in friends for a month, with no periods of elation. Option c is right because unipolar depression is both the most common of these disorders and the one closest to ordinary experience. Exam trap: 'bipolar' also involves depression and sounds like the bigger category, but bipolar disorder is far rarer and its defining feature is mania.", "traps": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder (intrusive obsessions and rituals) has a lifetime prevalence of about 2 percent, and its symptoms are not something almost everyone experiences.", "b": "Bipolar disorder includes depression but is defined by manic or hypomanic episodes; bipolar I affects only about 1 percent, and most people never experience mania.", "d": "Paranoid schizophrenia (a subtype dropped in DSM-5) involves delusions of persecution and hallucinations; schizophrenia affects about 1 percent and is far removed from everyday experience."}}, {"q": "Type of personality disorder characterized by always having to be the center of attention:", "concept": "The answer is d: needing always to be the centre of attention is the hallmark of histrionic personality disorder. In DSM-5 it belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic group (with antisocial, borderline and narcissistic personality disorders). It is a pervasive pattern of excessive emotionality and attention seeking, beginning by early adulthood and shown by at least five of eight features. The first listed is being uncomfortable in situations where one is not the centre of attention. The others are inappropriately seductive or provocative behaviour, rapidly shifting and shallow emotions, using physical appearance to draw attention, speech that is impressionistic and lacking in detail, theatrical and exaggerated emotional expression, suggestibility, and seeing relationships as more intimate than they really are. The name comes from the Latin histrio, actor, and the diagnosis grew out of the older idea of the 'hysterical personality'. Example: Riya arrives at a cousin's wedding in a striking outfit, tells a dramatic story with tears and laughter, and sulks when attention turns to the bride; she calls a man she met once her 'closest friend'. Option d is right because constant attention seeking is the core, and first, criterion of histrionic personality disorder. Exam trap: narcissistic personality disorder also craves attention, but the narcissist wants admiration for being superior, while the histrionic person wants attention of any kind, even through drama or illness; borderline is the Cluster B neighbour that examiners most often swap in.", "traps": {"a": "Borderline personality disorder (Cluster B) is instability of relationships, self-image and mood, with frantic fear of abandonment, impulsivity and self-harm; attention seeking can occur but is not its defining feature.", "b": "Antisocial personality disorder (Cluster B) is a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights, present from age 15 and diagnosed only from age 18; it is about exploitation, not attention.", "c": "Paranoid personality disorder (Cluster A) is pervasive distrust and suspicion, reading others' motives as hostile; such people tend to avoid the spotlight rather than seek it."}}, {"q": "Increased dopamine neurotransmitter receptors in the brain could be responsible for the onset of:", "concept": "The answer is b: increased dopamine receptors, and too much dopamine activity in general, are linked to schizophrenia. This is the dopamine hypothesis, the best-known biochemical theory of the disorder. Arvid Carlsson (Nobel Prize, 2000) showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter in its own right. The hypothesis rests mainly on drug evidence. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) found that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, calmed psychotic patients, and Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) argued that it works by blocking dopamine receptors. In the mid-1970s the laboratories of Philip Seeman and Solomon Snyder showed that the clinical potency of the typical antipsychotics closely tracks how strongly they bind to D2 receptors. In the other direction, amphetamine, which raises dopamine activity, can produce a paranoid psychosis in healthy people. Post-mortem and PET studies reported increased D2 receptor density in some patients. The revised version says that excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations), while a shortage in the mesocortical pathway contributes to negative and cognitive symptoms. Example: Kabir, after weeks of heavy amphetamine use, began hearing voices and believing he was being followed, a drug-induced picture that mimics paranoid schizophrenia. Option b is right because excess dopamine activity at its receptors is the classic biochemical account of schizophrenia. Exam trap: depression is also explained by neurotransmitters, so students pick it; but the classic theory of depression is a shortage of monoamines (noradrenaline and serotonin), the opposite direction.", "traps": {"a": "Depression is linked by the monoamine hypothesis to too little noradrenaline and serotonin (Joseph Schildkraut's 1965 catecholamine version stressed noradrenaline), the reverse direction from the dopamine excess of schizophrenia.", "c": "Anxiety is linked mainly to weak GABA inhibition (the target of benzodiazepines) and to serotonin and noradrenaline systems, not to an excess of dopamine receptors.", "d": "Phobias are explained mainly by learning (classical conditioning, as in Little Albert: John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner, 1920) and an overactive amygdala fear circuit, not by dopamine excess."}}, {"q": "What is culture bound disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: a culture-bound disorder (culture-bound syndrome) is a pattern of distress and behaviour that is recognised mainly within a particular cultural context. The term was introduced by the Hong Kong psychiatrist Pow Meng Yap in the 1960s for syndromes that seemed tied to local beliefs. Each has its own name, explanation and expected course in that culture. Examples: dhat syndrome in South Asia, anxiety, weakness and fatigue blamed on loss of semen, named by N. N. Wig (1960); koro in South and East Asia, the fear that the genitals are shrinking into the body; amok in Malaysia, brooding followed by a sudden outburst of violence; ataque de nervios in Latin America; and taijin kyofusho in Japan, fear of offending others by one's appearance or manner. DSM-IV (1994) listed such syndromes in a glossary in its appendix. DSM-5 (2013) renamed them cultural concepts of distress, because all distress is shaped by culture, and added the Cultural Formulation Interview to help clinicians ask about it. Example: Arjun, 19, from a village in Bihar, complains of weakness and poor memory and is sure it is because semen is passing in his urine; a clinician who knows only Western categories may miss that this is dhat. Option a is right because the defining feature of these syndromes is that they belong to a specific cultural setting. Exam trap: option b sounds similar but says the opposite; a disorder found in most cultures is universal, not culture-bound.", "traps": {"b": "A disorder that occurs in most cultures is universal (depression, schizophrenia); option b reverses the meaning of 'bound', which means tied to one culture.", "c": "Several culture-bound syndromes (dhat, koro, amok, susto) were listed in DSM-IV's glossary, and DSM-5 keeps several (dhat and susto among them) in its glossary of cultural concepts of distress, so they are not defined as disorders left out of the DSM.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, a disorder specific to a particular cultural context, is the standard definition."}}, {"q": "The most common focus of obsessive thoughts is:", "concept": "The answer is c: fear of dirt and contamination is the most common focus of obsessive thoughts. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety; compulsions are repetitive acts or mental acts performed to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event. They come in recognisable themes. In a large clinical study of about 560 patients with OCD, Steven Rasmussen and Jane Eisen (1992) found contamination obsessions in about 50 percent, pathological doubt in about 42 percent, somatic obsessions in 33, need for symmetry in 32, aggressive obsessions in 31 and sexual obsessions in 24. Most patients had more than one theme. Each theme tends to pair with a compulsion: contamination with washing and cleaning, doubt with checking, symmetry with ordering and counting. Later research grouped the symptoms into similar dimensions: contamination and washing, harm and checking, symmetry and ordering, and taboo (aggressive, sexual, religious) thoughts. Example: Riya, after touching a door handle, fears she will pass deadly germs to her mother and washes her hands until they are raw. Option c is right because contamination is the single most frequent obsession in clinical samples. Exam trap: repeated doubts (checking the stove or the lock) are also very common and come a close second, which makes d the tempting near miss. Key note: pathological doubt comes a close second in the Rasmussen and Eisen study, but contamination leads, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Sexual obsessions (unwanted sexual thoughts or images) belong to the taboo-thought dimension and were reported by about a quarter of patients, well behind contamination.", "b": "Aggressive obsessions (fear of harming someone, unwanted violent images) occur in about 30 percent of patients, a large group but not the most common one.", "d": "Pathological doubt (did I lock the door, turn off the gas?) drives checking and is the close second at about 42 percent, behind contamination at about 50 percent."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a criterion for PTSD?", "concept": "The answer is a: 'three or more dissociative symptoms' is not a criterion for PTSD; it was the DSM-IV requirement for acute stress disorder. Both disorders follow exposure to a traumatic event involving actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence. DSM-5 (2013) defines PTSD by four symptom clusters lasting more than one month: (1) intrusion, re-experiencing the event through memories, nightmares or flashbacks; (2) avoidance of reminders; (3) negative alterations in cognition and mood, including detachment from others, emotional numbing and inability to feel positive emotions; and (4) alterations in arousal and reactivity, such as hypervigilance, irritability and an exaggerated startle response. DSM-5 offers an optional 'with dissociative symptoms' specifier (depersonalisation or derealisation), but dissociation is not required. Acute stress disorder covers the first days to weeks after trauma. In DSM-IV it lasted 2 days to 4 weeks and required at least three of five dissociative symptoms: numbing or detachment, reduced awareness of surroundings, derealisation, depersonalisation and dissociative amnesia. In DSM-5 it lasts 3 days to 1 month and needs 9 of 14 symptoms from any category. Example: Kabir survived a train accident; three months later he has nightmares, avoids stations, feels cut off from his family and jumps at every loud noise. That is PTSD, with no dissociation needed. Option a is right because a required count of dissociative symptoms belongs to acute stress disorder, not PTSD. Exam trap: emotional numbing (c) sounds dissociative, so students mark it as the odd one out; but numbing is a PTSD symptom, listed under negative alterations in cognition and mood.", "traps": {"b": "Re-experiencing the event (intrusive memories, nightmares, flashbacks) is a core PTSD criterion, the intrusion cluster in DSM-5.", "c": "Emotional numbing and detachment from others are PTSD symptoms, listed in DSM-IV's avoidance and numbing cluster and in DSM-5's negative alterations in cognition and mood.", "d": "An exaggerated startle response is a PTSD criterion in the arousal and reactivity cluster, along with hypervigilance, irritability and poor sleep."}}, {"q": "CBT is generally perceived as:", "concept": "The answer is d: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is generally seen as evidence based. CBT combines the cognitive therapies, the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (from 1955) and the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (from the 1960s), with the techniques of behaviour therapy. It holds that distorted thoughts keep distressing emotions and unhelpful behaviour going, and it treats them by identifying, testing and changing those thoughts, alongside behavioural methods such as exposure and activity scheduling. Several features made it easy to test: it is structured, time-limited and problem-focused, often follows a manual, and tracks progress with standard scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory. As a result it is the most researched psychotherapy. Dianne Chambless and Steven Hollon (1998) set criteria for empirically supported treatments, and CBT for depression and several anxiety disorders met them. Andrew Butler, Jason Chapman, Evan Forman and Aaron Beck (2006) reviewed 16 meta-analyses and found large effects for depression, generalised anxiety, panic, social phobia and PTSD. In the UK, NICE guidelines recommend it as a first-line treatment for depression and most anxiety disorders. Example: Arjun, who panics before presentations, keeps a thought record, tests his prediction that everyone will laugh, and his anxiety falls over 12 sessions while a weekly rating scale tracks the change. Option d is right because CBT's reputation rests above all on its research support. Exam trap: CBT does work with the client's thoughts, so 'phenomenological' tempts; but phenomenology means valuing subjective experience as it is lived, whereas CBT tests thoughts against evidence.", "traps": {"a": "Phenomenological describes approaches centred on the client's subjective, lived experience, such as Carl Rogers's person-centred therapy (1951) and existential therapy; CBT instead tests thoughts against evidence.", "b": "Psychodynamic therapy (Sigmund Freud, from the 1890s, and his successors) seeks insight into unconscious conflicts and early relationships; CBT works on present, conscious thoughts and behaviours.", "c": "Humanistic therapy (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) stresses growth, free will and the therapy relationship; CBT is a directive, skills-based approach built on learning and cognitive models."}}, {"q": "Behavioural analysis is based upon the principles of:", "concept": "The answer is b: behaviour analysis is based on the principles of operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938), holds that behaviour is shaped by its consequences: reinforcement makes a behaviour more likely, while punishment and extinction make it less likely. Skinner called the laboratory study of these principles the experimental analysis of behaviour. Applied behaviour analysis (ABA) carries them into real life: Donald Baer, Montrose Wolf and Todd Risley (1968), in the first issue of the Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, defined it as applying behavioural principles to socially important behaviour and measuring the change. Its core tool is functional analysis: recording the antecedent, the behaviour and the consequence (the ABC) to find out what the behaviour gets the person or lets him escape. Treatment then changes the consequences through shaping, reinforcement schedules, extinction and token economies. ABA is best known in the treatment of autism. Example: Kabir, aged 6, throws tantrums during homework; a functional analysis shows the tantrums get him out of work, so the plan stops letting tantrums end homework and praises him for asking politely for a break. Option b is right because behaviour analysis is Skinner's operant tradition. Exam trap: behaviour therapy as a whole also uses classical conditioning, so students choose a or 'All of the above'. Key note: behaviour therapy also uses classical conditioning (Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, 1958), but 'behaviour analysis' names Skinner's operant tradition, and 'All of the above' fails because dream analysis is psychoanalytic, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, Conditioned Reflexes, 1927) pairs stimuli and underlies methods such as Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation; it belongs to behaviour therapy broadly, but behaviour analysis is built on consequences, Skinner's operant principles.", "c": "Dream analysis is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic method (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900) for uncovering unconscious wishes; behaviour analysis rejects inferred inner causes and works only with observable behaviour.", "d": "All of the above fails because dream analysis has no place in behaviour analysis, and classical conditioning, though used in behaviour therapy, is not the basis of the analysis of behaviour."}}, {"q": "A central goal of person-centered therapy is to:", "concept": "The answer is c: a central goal of person-centred therapy is to promote congruence between the person's experienced self and ideal self. Carl Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951; On Becoming a Person, 1961) held that problems arise from incongruence. A child who receives only conditional regard takes in conditions of worth, then denies or distorts experiences that do not fit the self-concept. The result is a gap between the self-concept and actual experience, and often a wide gap between the self as the person experiences it and the ideal self he feels he must become. Therapy narrows these gaps. Rogers (1957) argued that if the therapist offers the core conditions of congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard and accurate empathy, the client can safely admit denied feelings, drop the conditions of worth and move towards being a fully functioning person. His team measured change with the Q-sort (William Stephenson, 1953): clients sorted self-descriptive statements once for 'how I am' and once for 'how I would like to be'. John Butler and Gerard Haigh (1954) found that the average self and ideal-self correlation rose from about zero before client-centred therapy to about .3 after it. Example: Meera feels she should be the dutiful daughter but longs to paint; in therapy she accepts both sides, and her self and ideal self move closer. Option c is right because reducing incongruence is the textbook goal of person-centred therapy. Exam trap: option b sounds like 'living up to ideals', but introjected ideals are the conditions of worth that cause the problem. Key note: strictly Rogers spoke of congruence between the self-concept and experience, and the narrowing self and ideal-self gap was how his team measured progress; option c is the textbook phrasing, so it stands.", "traps": {"a": "Strengthening defence mechanisms is closer to supportive, ego-strengthening therapy; Rogers wanted clients to need fewer defences, so that denied and distorted experiences could enter awareness.", "b": "Introjected ideals are the conditions of worth taken in from others, which Rogers saw as the cause of incongruence; helping clients live up to them would deepen the problem rather than resolve it.", "d": "Looking at oneself objectively and realistically is the aim of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1976), which tests thoughts against evidence; Rogers valued the client's own subjective experience, not an outside objective view."}}, {"q": "______ is a term that is used when clients believe that they have more control over potentially negative outcomes than they actually do.", "concept": "The answer is c: reattribution of responsibility is the cognitive therapy term used when clients believe they have more control over, and so more responsibility for, potentially negative outcomes than they actually do. It belongs to the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (Cognitive Therapy and the Emotional Disorders, 1976). Beck held that depression and anxiety are kept going by distorted automatic thoughts. One such distortion is personalisation: taking personal blame for events that have many causes, most of them outside one's control. Reattribution is the technique that corrects it. Therapist and client list every factor that contributed to the outcome, estimate how much each played a part, and share out responsibility realistically, sometimes by drawing a 'responsibility pie'. The aim is not to deny all responsibility, but to stop the client carrying all of it. Example: Kabir blames himself entirely because his younger brother failed his board exams, believing he should have been able to prevent it. In reattribution he lists the other factors (his brother's own effort, an illness before the exam, a hard paper) and sees that his share was small, and his guilt eases. Option c is right because the stem describes inflated control and self-blame for negative outcomes, which reattribution of responsibility is designed to address. Exam trap: overgeneralisation and catastrophising are also on Beck's list of cognitive errors, so they sound plausible; but neither concerns how much control or blame the client assigns to himself.", "traps": {"a": "Overgeneralisation is one of Beck's cognitive distortions: drawing a sweeping rule from a single event ('I failed once, so I always fail'); it is about generalising, not about overestimating one's control over outcomes.", "b": "Catastrophising (catastrophic thinking) is the distortion of expecting the worst possible outcome; it exaggerates how bad things will be, not how much the client controls them.", "d": "Generating alternative interpretations is a different cognitive technique in which the client looks for other explanations of an event (perhaps a friend did not reply because she was busy); it is not the name for overestimated control."}}, {"q": "Fundamental difference between various psychotherapeutic approaches is:", "concept": "The answer is c: what fundamentally separates one psychotherapeutic approach from another is its theoretical orientation. A theoretical orientation is the set of assumptions a therapy makes about human nature, about how psychological problems develop and about how change happens. Those assumptions decide what the therapist does. Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (from the 1890s) sees problems as unconscious childhood conflict, so the analyst interprets and builds insight. Behaviour therapy sees problems as learned habits, so the therapist arranges new learning through exposure or reinforcement. Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (from the 1960s) sees faulty thinking, so client and therapist test thoughts against evidence. Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy (1951) sees blocked growth, so the therapist offers empathy and acceptance. Example: Meera has exam anxiety. A psychodynamic therapist explores her fear of disappointing her father; a behaviour therapist exposes her step by step to mock tests; a cognitive therapist challenges her belief \"one bad mark means I am a failure\". Same client, same problem, different theory, so a different therapy. Option c is right because theory is the defining feature of an approach and its techniques follow from it; length, scope and client base vary inside every approach. Exam trap: the common factors view says bona fide therapies give roughly equal results through shared ingredients such as the alliance, empathy and hope. Saul Rosenzweig (1936) first argued this, quoting the Dodo in Alice in Wonderland (\"everybody has won, and all must have prizes\"), and Bruce Wampold (The Great Psychotherapy Debate, 2001) developed it. That is a claim about outcomes, not about what defines an approach: the approaches still differ in theory even when their results are similar.", "traps": {"a": "Length of treatment is a practical feature that varies inside every approach (classical analysis can run for years, brief psychodynamic therapy and CBT are time-limited); it says how long therapy lasts, not what the approach believes.", "b": "Scope of practice is the legal and professional limit on what a practitioner (psychologist, counsellor, psychiatrist) is trained and licensed to do; it belongs to the profession, not to a therapy approach.", "d": "Client base is the group a therapist serves (children, couples, older adults); almost any approach can be used with many client groups, so it does not define the approach."}}, {"q": "Things such as socio-cultural values, political movements, historical events, economic features are known as ______ within which counselling theories originate.", "concept": "The answer is b: socio-cultural values, political movements, historical events and economic conditions make up the context within which counselling theories originate. No theory is born in a vacuum; each reflects the time, place and people that produced it. Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900) built psychoanalysis while treating middle-class patients in Vienna around 1900, a society that kept open talk of sex under tight control, and his theory put repressed sexual wishes at its centre. Carl Rogers (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942) developed his non-directive, later client-centred, approach in an individualistic, democratic America that prized self-direction. Feminist therapy grew out of the women's movement of the 1960s and 1970s, and multicultural counselling out of the civil rights era. John Sommers-Flanagan and Rita Sommers-Flanagan made this the organising theme of their textbook Counseling and Psychotherapy Theories in Context and Practice (2004). Example: Arjun, from a joint family in Lucknow, makes career decisions together with his parents. A counsellor who knows that most Western theories grew in individualistic cultures will not rush to call this dependence or enmeshment. Option b is right because \"context\" is the term for this surrounding web of social, political, historical and economic forces. Exam trap: \"Factors\" sounds plausible, because these are indeed influencing factors, but the word used for the setting in which a theory arises is context; and \"schools\" are the theories themselves, not the background they grow out of.", "traps": {"a": "Factors is a general word for any influence (risk factors, the common factors of therapy); it is not the term for the social and historical setting in which a theory arises.", "c": "Boundaries in counselling are the ethical limits of the therapist and client relationship (no dual relationships, no sexual contact, limits on gifts); they have nothing to do with where theories come from.", "d": "Schools are the theories or traditions themselves (the psychoanalytic, behavioural or humanistic school); the stem asks for the setting a school grows out of, not the school."}}, {"q": "Every psychotherapeutic approach has two components:", "concept": "The answer is a: every psychotherapeutic approach has two components, a theory and a practice. The theory is the explanation: how personality develops, how problems arise and what must change for the person to get better. The practice is what the therapist actually does: the techniques, the kind of relationship offered and the structure of sessions, all of which follow from the theory. Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (begun in 1955 as rational therapy) holds in theory that irrational beliefs, not events, cause emotional upset (the ABC model); in practice the therapist disputes those beliefs and sets homework. Joseph Wolpe's (1958) theory that anxiety is a conditioned response leads in practice to systematic desensitisation. Between the two sits case conceptualisation (case formulation): applying the theory to one client to explain why this person has these problems now and to plan treatment. Evaluation then checks whether the practice worked. Example: Kabir's counsellor follows Aaron Beck's cognitive theory (from the 1960s). She conceptualises his low mood as driven by the belief \"nobody likes me\", then in practice uses thought records and behavioural experiments to test it. Option a is right because theory and practice are the two pillars on which every approach stands; the pairing is also the theme of John Sommers-Flanagan and Rita Sommers-Flanagan's textbook Counseling and Psychotherapy Theories in Context and Practice (2004). Exam trap: case conceptualisation and evaluation are real clinical terms, so options that pair them with theory or practice look sophisticated, but each one swaps a sub-step for one of the two basic components.", "traps": {"b": "Case conceptualisation is the bridge that applies a theory to one client; pairing it with practice leaves out the theory that the conceptualisation itself depends on.", "c": "Theory and case conceptualisation leaves out practice, the techniques and relationship through which therapy actually helps; conceptualisation is an application of theory, not a separate pillar.", "d": "Evaluation (measuring outcome) is a later step that checks whether practice worked; practice and evaluation leave out the theory that tells the therapist what to do and why."}}, {"q": "Which of the following are the only therapeutic approaches that existed in the first half of the 20th century?", "concept": "The answer is b: in the first half of the 20th century the established systems of therapy were Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and Alfred Adler's individual psychology (Adlerian therapy). Freud developed psychoanalysis in Vienna from the 1890s (Studies on Hysteria with Josef Breuer, 1895; The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900); free association, dream interpretation and analysis of transference uncover repressed childhood conflict. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937), once in Freud's circle, broke away in 1911 and founded individual psychology. He put feelings of inferiority, striving for superiority, social interest and the style of life in place of sexual drives, and made therapy an equal, educational partnership that explores birth order and early memories. The other approaches in the options came later. Carl Rogers set out non-directive counselling in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942); it became client-centred in 1951 and person-centred in the 1970s. Gestalt therapy was launched in 1951 by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman in Gestalt Therapy. Behaviour therapy (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) and the cognitive therapies (Albert Ellis, 1955; Aaron Beck, 1960s) belong to the second half. Example: Riya draws a timeline for revision; psychoanalysis and Adlerian therapy go in the pre-1950 column, and the person-centred, Gestalt, behavioural and cognitive therapies go in the 1940s to 1960s columns. Option b is right because it is the only pair in which both approaches were already full therapy systems in that period. Exam trap: every wrong option contains Gestalt or person-centred therapy, which are mid-century, and Carl Jung (who broke with Freud in 1913) tempts because he was Freud's contemporary. Key note: Jung's analytical psychology dates from 1913 and Rogers's non-directive counselling from 1942, so the word \"only\" is too strong and option c has some case; but psychoanalysis and Adlerian therapy are the pair counselling texts present as the established approaches of that period, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Jung's analytical psychology did exist early (after his 1913 break with Freud), but Gestalt therapy was founded only in 1951 by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, so the pair fails.", "c": "Jungian analysis is early and Carl Rogers's non-directive counselling began in 1942, but person-centred therapy as a named system belongs to the 1950s to 1970s; the early established pair is psychoanalytic and Adlerian.", "d": "Adlerian therapy fits, but person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers: non-directive 1942, client-centred 1951, person-centred from the 1970s) belongs to the mid-century humanistic wave, not the early period."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of an untestable hypothesis?", "concept": "The answer is d: non-directionality is not a characteristic of an untestable hypothesis, because a non-directional hypothesis is perfectly testable. A hypothesis is testable when some possible observation could show it to be wrong; Karl Popper (1934) made this falsifiability the mark of a scientific claim. John Shaughnessy, Eugene Zechmeister and Jeanne Zechmeister, in their textbook Research Methods in Psychology, list three marks of an untestable hypothesis. First, the concepts are not adequately defined: \"stress harms memory\" cannot be tested until stress and memory have operational definitions (stress as a cortisol level, memory as words recalled from a list). Second, circularity: the event is used as its own explanation. \"Kabir is lazy because he does not work, and we know he is lazy because he does not work\" can never be disproved. Third, an appeal to ideas or forces not recognised by science, such as ghosts, fate or bad luck, which science has no way to measure. A non-directional (two-tailed) hypothesis predicts that a difference or relationship exists without saying which way: \"boys and girls will differ in reading speed\". A directional (one-tailed) hypothesis names the direction: \"girls will read faster\". Both can be tested; the non-directional one simply uses both tails of the distribution. Option d is right because direction concerns how a hypothesis is worded, not whether it can be tested. Exam trap: students link \"non-directional\" with \"vague\" and so with \"untestable\", but a hypothesis is untestable only when its terms are undefined, its logic is circular or it relies on forces outside science, not when it leaves out the direction of the effect.", "traps": {"a": "Appeal to unscientific notions (ghosts, fate, forces science does not recognise) is one of the three marks of an untestable hypothesis, because no observation can measure or rule them out.", "b": "Inadequate definition of concepts is a mark of an untestable hypothesis: without operational definitions nobody knows what result would count against it.", "c": "Circularity, using the event as its own explanation, is a mark of an untestable hypothesis, since the claim is true by definition and can never be disproved."}}, {"q": "One of the preoccupations of quantitative researchers is with generalization which is an indicator of:", "concept": "The answer is a: generalisation, one of the main preoccupations of quantitative researchers, is an indicator of external validity. Alan Bryman (Social Research Methods, 2001) lists four preoccupations of quantitative research: measurement (are concepts measured reliably and validly?), causality (does X really cause Y?), generalisation (do the findings apply beyond the people and setting studied?) and replication (could another researcher repeat the study and get the same result?). External validity is the extent to which results generalise to other people (population validity), other settings (ecological validity) and other times. Internal validity is the confidence that the independent variable, and not something else, caused the change in the dependent variable. Donald Campbell (1957) first set internal against external validity, and with Julian Stanley (1963) listed the threats to each. Quantitative researchers pursue generalisation mainly through random sampling from a defined population, which lets findings be extended to that population with known error. Example: Meera surveys 200 randomly chosen college students in Pune about exam stress. If her result holds for college students across Maharashtra, her study has good external validity; if it is true only of her own college, it does not. Option a is right because \"can the findings be generalised?\" is exactly the question external validity asks. Exam trap: the options cross two words (internal, external) with two others (validity, reliability). Drawing on Margaret LeCompte and Judith Goetz (1982), Bryman uses external reliability for whether a study can be replicated; internal reliability means the items of a scale (or, in qualitative work, the observers) agree. So replication goes with reliability, causality with internal validity and generalisation with external validity.", "traps": {"b": "External reliability is whether a study can be replicated with the same results (a term Margaret LeCompte and Judith Goetz used for qualitative research); it matches Bryman's replication, not generalisation.", "c": "Internal validity is confidence that the independent variable caused the change in the dependent variable; it matches Bryman's causality, not whether findings extend beyond the sample.", "d": "Internal reliability is the consistency of the items that make up a scale (Cronbach's alpha) or, in qualitative work, agreement among observers; it concerns measurement, not generalisation."}}, {"q": "Measurement scale which allows researchers and statisticians to perform certain operations on data collected from respondents is classified as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the interval scale is the measurement scale that lets researchers and statisticians perform arithmetic operations such as addition and subtraction on the data they collect. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding a property. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, religion, a jersey number). An ordinal scale ranks them (class rank, socio-economic class), but the gaps between ranks are unknown. An interval scale adds equal units: the rise from 20 to 30 degrees Celsius is the same as from 30 to 40, so adding, subtracting and averaging make sense, and means, standard deviations and Pearson's r can be computed. Its zero is arbitrary, a reference point and not the absence of the quantity, so ratios are meaningless: 40 degrees Celsius is not twice as hot as 20. A ratio scale adds a true zero (height, weight, reaction time, income), so multiplication and division work as well. Example: Arjun scores 110 and Riya 100 on an IQ test treated as interval data; Arjun is 10 points higher, but he is not 10 percent more intelligent. Option a is right because interval is the only true level of measurement among the options and the first level at which arithmetic on the numbers is meaningful. Exam trap: \"validity scale\" and \"reliability scale\" sound like measurement scales, but validity and reliability are properties of tests; the MMPI's validity scales (L, F and K) detect test-taking attitudes. Key note: the ratio scale allows even more operations (multiplication, division, ratios) and the stem is vague, but interval is the only level of measurement among the options (flow-measuring scales are engineering instruments), so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "A flow-measuring scale is an engineering instrument (a flow meter) for liquids or gases; it is not one of Stevens's levels of measurement.", "c": "Validity is a property of a test (does it measure what it claims to?); the validity scales of inventories such as the MMPI (L, F, K) detect lying or careless answering and are not a level of measurement.", "d": "Reliability is the consistency of a test's scores (test-retest, split-half, Cronbach's alpha); there is no \"reliability scale\" among the levels of measurement."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a threat to the internal validity of an experiment?", "concept": "The answer is b: a within-groups design is not a threat to internal validity; it is a research design. Internal validity is the confidence that the independent variable (IV), and nothing else, caused the change in the dependent variable (DV), and a threat is any rival explanation. Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed eight classic threats: history, maturation, testing, instrumentation, statistical regression, selection, experimental mortality (drop-out) and selection-maturation interaction. The other options are threats too. A confounding variable changes along with the IV, so its effect cannot be separated from the IV's. Demand characteristics, named by Martin Orne (1962), are cues that tell participants what the experimenter expects, so they act out the hypothesis. Experimenter effects are the researcher's expectations shaping the results: Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode (1963) told students their rats were \"maze-bright\" or \"maze-dull\", and the \"bright\" rats, though labelled at random, learned faster. A within-groups (repeated-measures) design tests the same people in every condition. That removes individual differences as a confound, which strengthens internal validity. It brings order effects (practice, fatigue, carryover), but these are handled by counterbalancing, varying the order of conditions across participants. Example: Kabir tests whether music helps memory; every participant learns one list in silence and one with music, half doing silence first and half music first. Option b is right because a design is a way of controlling variables, not a rival explanation for the results. Exam trap: order effects tempt students to call the whole design a threat; the threat is an uncontrolled order effect, not the design itself.", "traps": {"a": "Demand characteristics (Martin Orne, 1962) are cues that reveal the hypothesis, so participants behave as they think they should; this is a rival explanation and so a threat to internal validity.", "c": "Experimenter effects (Robert Rosenthal, 1966) are the researcher's expectations or behaviour shaping the results, as in the maze-bright rats study; they threaten internal validity, which is why double-blind procedures are used.", "d": "A confounding variable varies along with the IV, so the change in the DV could be due to it instead; it is the textbook threat to internal validity."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an accurate criticism of projective tests of personality?", "concept": "The answer is b: an accurate criticism of projective tests is that they require too many inferences on the part of the examiner and do not show high validity. A projective test presents an ambiguous stimulus, such as an inkblot (Hermann Rorschach, 1921), a vague picture (the Thematic Apperception Test of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) or an unfinished sentence, and assumes the person projects needs and conflicts into the answer. Because answers are free and open, the examiner must infer what each response means, and different examiners can read the same record differently. The main criticisms follow from this. Interscorer reliability is often weak unless a structured system such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) is used; test-retest reliability is low, because stories change from one occasion to the next; and validity for most purposes, including diagnosis, is poor, with little gain over an interview and a questionnaire. Scott Lilienfeld, James Wood and Howard Garb (2000) reviewed the evidence and found support for only a few indices, such as Rorschach measures of thought disorder. Loren Chapman and Jean Chapman (1967, 1969) showed how clinicians come to trust invalid signs through illusory correlation. Example: two psychologists score Riya's TAT stories; one sees hidden aggression, the other sees achievement striving. That disagreement is the inference problem. Option b is right because it names both core problems: heavy examiner inference and weak validity. Exam trap: option a is half right, since poor interjudge reliability is a real criticism, but it adds \"high validity\"; reliability limits validity, so a test that scorers cannot agree on cannot be highly valid.", "traps": {"a": "Poor interjudge reliability is a genuine criticism, but \"high validity\" is wrong: projective tests show weak validity for most uses, and an unreliable test cannot be highly valid.", "c": "This reverses the criticism: projective tests are faulted for low reliability (interscorer and test-retest), not for reliability that is too high.", "d": "Projective tests are designed to let people express themselves freely through ambiguous stimuli and open answers; preventing self-expression is the opposite of how they work."}}, {"q": "Rorschach Inkblot Test was standardised on which method?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Rorschach Inkblot Test was standardised by John Exner's method, the Comprehensive System (1974). Hermann Rorschach (Psychodiagnostik, 1921) published ten inkblots but died in 1922 without leaving a full scoring system. In the United States five rival systems grew up: those of Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski, and David Rapaport with Roy Schafer. Each coded responses differently, so research findings could not be compared. John Exner (1928 to 2006) studied all five, kept the elements with the best research support and combined them into one standardised procedure for administration, scoring and interpretation. His Structural Summary turns the codes (location, determinants such as form, colour and movement, form quality, content) into ratios and indices compared against norms, such as the Depression Index and the Suicide Constellation. A valid record needs at least 14 responses. After his death Gregory Meyer and colleagues published a successor, the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011). Example: Arjun's Rorschach is scored in a hospital in Bengaluru and again in one in Delhi; because both follow Exner's rules and norms, the two results can be compared. Option a is right because Exner's Comprehensive System is the method that standardised the Rorschach. Exam trap: Henry Murray is the name tied to a different projective test, the Thematic Apperception Test, which he created with Christiana Morgan (1935) and scored with his need-press system; it has nothing to do with the Rorschach.", "traps": {"b": "Murray's system belongs to the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), scored for the hero's needs and the presses of the environment; it was never a Rorschach scoring system.", "c": "Both is wrong because only Exner's Comprehensive System standardised the Rorschach; Murray's need-press scoring is for the TAT.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, Exner's Comprehensive System (1974), is the standard Rorschach method."}}, {"q": "What is Rorschach Inkblot Test designed to measure?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Rorschach Inkblot Test is designed to measure unconscious motives and intentions, the hidden needs, conflicts and ways of seeing that a person cannot easily report. Hermann Rorschach, a Swiss psychiatrist, published it in Psychodiagnostik (1921). It has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, always shown in the same order: five achromatic (black and grey), two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be, and the examiner then goes back through the cards in an inquiry to learn where and why each percept was seen. The test rests on the projective hypothesis: when a stimulus is ambiguous and has no right answer, the structure a person puts into it must come from inside the person. Lawrence Frank (1939) gave these methods the name projective. So a response is taken to reveal personality structure, conflicts and unconscious processes that the person is not aware of and could not report on a questionnaire. Responses are scored for location (whole blot or detail), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), form quality and content. Example: Kabir sees \"two people pulling something apart\" in several cards; the examiner treats a repeated theme like this as a clue to his inner conflicts, not as a statement he chose to make. Option c is right because uncovering what lies outside awareness is the stated purpose of the test. Exam trap: conscious desires are what self-report inventories such as the MMPI or the 16PF tap; the Rorschach was built because people cannot or will not report what is unconscious.", "traps": {"a": "Dreams are interpreted in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis as disguised wishes; the Rorschach asks a waking person to interpret inkblots and does not record or measure dreams.", "b": "Conscious desires are what people can report directly, which is the job of self-report questionnaires; the Rorschach targets what lies outside awareness.", "d": "Brain size is measured by neuroimaging such as MRI (phrenology once claimed to read the size of mental organs from bumps on the skull); an inkblot test cannot measure it."}}, {"q": "Psychologists working with hospitalized mental patients may find ______ helpful to record the frequency of patients' aggression, self-care, speech and unusual behaviour.", "concept": "The answer is a: psychologists working with hospitalised mental patients may find behavioural assessment helpful for recording how often patients show aggression, self-care, speech and unusual behaviour. Behavioural assessment grew with behaviour therapy in the 1960s and 1970s. Instead of inferring hidden traits, it measures specific target behaviours directly, where they happen, in terms of frequency, duration and intensity, usually before and during treatment. Each behaviour first gets an operational definition (for example, aggression means hitting, kicking or throwing objects at a person). Staff then record it by event recording (counting each occurrence) or by interval recording and time sampling (checking at set moments whether it is occurring), often on a ward checklist. Functional analysis, rooted in the operant conditioning of B. F. Skinner (1938), adds what comes before and after each behaviour: antecedent, behaviour, consequence (the ABC). A classic ward example is Teodoro Ayllon and Jack Michael (1959), \"The psychiatric nurse as a behavioral engineer\": nurses recorded the frequency of patients' problem behaviours and then changed their consequences. The token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) rested on the same daily counts. Example: on a ward in Ranchi, nurses tally each time Sunil hits someone and each time he dresses himself; the counts show whether his treatment plan is working. Option a is right because counting how often specific behaviours occur is the defining method of behavioural assessment. Exam trap: rating scale and observation look right because they are real tools used on wards, but they are techniques inside behavioural assessment, not the approach the stem describes. Key note: rating scales and direct observation are the tools used inside behavioural assessment (a ward behaviour rating scale would also do the job), but the approach built around recording how often specific behaviours occur is behavioural assessment, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Personality questionnaires (the MMPI, the 16PF) are self-report inventories of traits filled in by the patient; they cannot record how often behaviours actually occur on the ward.", "c": "A rating scale (such as a ward behaviour rating scale) is one tool that can be used inside behavioural assessment, but it gives a rater's judgement of degree, not the direct frequency counts the stem describes.", "d": "Observation is the method behavioural assessment uses, but on its own it is a general way of collecting data; systematic counting of defined target behaviours over time is what makes it behavioural assessment."}}, {"q": "Non-sampling error is reduced by:", "concept": "The answer is b: non-sampling error is reduced by decreasing the sample size, in the sense that a smaller, well-controlled survey leaves fewer chances for such errors than a large survey or a complete census. Survey error has two parts. Sampling error is the chance gap between a sample statistic and the population parameter, caused only by which cases happen to be drawn. It shrinks as the sample grows, because the standard error equals the standard deviation divided by the square root of n, and it is zero in a census. Non-sampling errors are everything else: measurement and recording mistakes, badly worded questions, non-response, incomplete coverage of the population, interviewer bias, and coding and data-entry errors. They occur in a census too, and they tend to grow as the operation grows, because more respondents, more interviewers and more data handling mean more chances for mistakes and looser supervision. This is why statistics texts say a carefully run sample can be more accurate than a census. Example: Arjun's team must survey a district of 50,000 homes. Visiting every home needs hundreds of hastily trained enumerators and produces missed homes and coding slips; a sample of 2,000 homes covered by a few well-trained, closely supervised interviewers has some sampling error but far less non-sampling error. Option b is right because non-sampling error rises with the size of the survey, so a smaller, better-managed sample reduces it. Exam trap: option a is the remedy for sampling error; increasing n reduces sampling error but tends to increase non-sampling error. Key note: the real remedies for non-sampling error are careful design, pre-testing, trained field staff, follow-up of non-respondents and data checking, so \"None of these\" has a case; but the textbook point this item tests is that non-sampling error rises with the size of the survey, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Increasing sample size reduces sampling error (the standard error falls with the square root of n), but it adds interviews and data handling, so non-sampling error tends to rise.", "c": "Reducing the amount of data is not a recognised remedy and would weaken the study; non-sampling error depends on how many cases are handled and how carefully, which is the point of option b.", "d": "None of these fails for this item because option b, a smaller and better-managed sample, is the textbook answer (alongside good design, training and data checking)."}}, {"q": "The difference between statistic and parameter is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the difference between a statistic and a parameter is sampling error. A parameter describes a whole population (the population mean, mu); a statistic describes a sample drawn from it (the sample mean, x-bar) and is used to estimate the parameter. Because a sample contains only some of the population, chance decides which cases are in it, so the statistic almost never equals the parameter exactly. That chance gap, statistic minus parameter, is sampling error. The American educational statistician Everet Franklin Lindquist defined it as the difference between a parameter and an estimate of that parameter derived from a sample. It implies no mistake by the researcher; it shrinks as the sample grows and is zero in a full census. If many samples are drawn, their statistics form a sampling distribution, and the standard deviation of that distribution is the standard error: the typical size of sampling error (for the mean, the standard deviation divided by the square root of n). Example: the true mean IQ of all 5,000 students in a Pune college is 102. Meera's random sample of 50 students has a mean of 99. The 3-point gap is the sampling error of her sample; another sample might miss by 1 or by 4. Option b is right because sampling error is defined as exactly this difference between a sample value and the population value. Exam trap: standard error is the closest distractor, but it is the standard deviation of such errors across repeated samples, a measure of their typical size, not the difference for one sample.", "traps": {"a": "Random error is unsystematic noise in measurement (a tired participant, a misread scale) that scatters scores around their true values; it is a measurement idea, not the gap between statistic and parameter.", "c": "Standard error is the standard deviation of a sampling distribution, the typical size of sampling error across many samples, not the difference itself for a given sample.", "d": "Error alone is too vague; the specific name for the gap between statistic and parameter is sampling error."}}, {"q": "Concerning \"authorship\" in educational research, intellectual ownership is predominantly a function of:", "concept": "The answer is b: in research, intellectual ownership, and so authorship, is mainly a function of creative contribution. Authorship belongs to the people who made a substantial scientific or professional contribution to the work: forming the idea or hypothesis, designing the study, analysing and interpreting the data, and writing the paper. The Ethics Code of the American Psychological Association (2002), Standard 8.12 on publication credit, says authorship should reflect the relative scientific or professional contributions of those involved, regardless of their status, and that holding an institutional position such as department head does not by itself justify authorship. Minor contributions, such as data entry, typing, routine help with recruitment or running a standard analysis, are recognised in an acknowledgement, not by authorship. The order of authors should follow the size of each person's contribution, and a student is usually listed first on a multi-author article based mainly on the student's own dissertation (Standard 8.12c). Adding a senior person who did nothing is gift (honorary) authorship and is unethical. Example: Riya thought of the study, designed it and wrote the paper; her supervisor helped plan the analysis; a lab assistant entered data for months. Riya is first author, the supervisor second, and the assistant is thanked in the acknowledgements, however many hours she put in. Option b is right because authorship follows intellectual and creative input, not effort, rank or degrees. Exam trap: effort expended feels fair, but long hours of routine work earn an acknowledgement, not authorship, while a short but decisive creative contribution can earn it.", "traps": {"a": "Effort expended is not the test: routine work such as data entry or typing, however time-consuming, is recognised in an acknowledgement, not by authorship.", "c": "Professional position does not create authorship; APA Standard 8.12 says holding an institutional post such as head of department does not by itself justify authorship credit.", "d": "Level of higher education is irrelevant: a student can be first author when the work is mainly theirs, and a senior professor earns no credit for a degree alone."}}, {"q": "Identify the term that refers to a post-study interview in which all aspects of the study are revealed, reasons for the use of deception are given, and the participants' questions are answered:", "concept": "The answer is b: the post-study interview in which all aspects of the study are revealed, the reasons for any deception are explained and participants' questions are answered is called debriefing. Some studies cannot reveal their true purpose in advance, because knowing it would change how participants behave. The Ethics Code of the American Psychological Association (Standard 8.08) therefore requires researchers to give participants a prompt chance to learn the nature, results and conclusions of the research, to correct any misconceptions, and to take steps to reduce any harm the procedure caused. David Holmes (1976) split debriefing into two parts. Dehoaxing convinces participants that the false information they were given was false. Desensitising helps them deal with any distress or unwelcome self-knowledge the study produced. In the obedience studies of Stanley Milgram (1963), participants afterwards met the \"learner\", unharmed, and were later sent a written report on the study (dehoaxing), and they were reassured that their behaviour was normal and shared by many others (desensitising). Example: Riya tells participants that a test measures intelligence when it really measures reactions to failure. Afterwards she explains the real aim, why she had to mislead them and that the failure feedback was fake, and answers their questions. That whole conversation is debriefing. Option b is right because debriefing is the name for the complete post-study interview the stem describes. Exam trap: dehoaxing and desensitising are real terms, but each is only one part of debriefing, while the stem describes the whole interview. Key note: dehoaxing and desensitising are both parts of debriefing, so the whole post-study interview described is debriefing, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Desensitising (David Holmes, 1976) is the part of debriefing that removes distress or harmful self-knowledge caused by the study; it is one component, not the whole interview.", "c": "Dehoaxing is the part of debriefing that convinces participants the deception was false (the learner received no shocks); revealing the deception alone is not the full interview with reasons and questions.", "d": "Deploying is not a research-ethics term at all; it is a filler word meaning to put something into action."}}, {"q": "Which of the following values could not represent a correlation coefficient?", "concept": "The answer is a: r = 1.09 could not be a correlation coefficient, because a correlation coefficient can only take values from minus 1 to plus 1. Karl Pearson (1896) gave the product-moment correlation coefficient, r, its formula: the covariance of two variables divided by the product of their standard deviations. Dividing by the standard deviations puts r on a fixed scale on which no relationship can be stronger than perfect. The sign shows direction: positive when both variables rise together, negative when one rises as the other falls. The size, ignoring the sign, shows strength: 0 means no linear relationship and 1 means a perfect one. So r = 1.0 is possible: a perfect positive correlation, with every point on a rising straight line (temperature in Celsius against the same temperature in Fahrenheit, for example). r = 0.99 is a very strong positive correlation, and r = minus 0.73 is a strong negative one, exactly as strong as plus 0.73 but in the opposite direction. Squaring r gives the coefficient of determination, so r = 0.99 means about 98 percent shared variance. Example: Kabir correlates study hours with marks and gets 1.09. He does not report a \"better than perfect\" relationship; he knows he has made a calculation error and checks his sums. Option a is right because 1.09 lies outside the possible range of r. Exam trap: students sometimes reject r = 1.0 as too perfect to be real; it is rare with real data, but it is a possible value, whereas anything above plus 1 or below minus 1 is impossible.", "traps": {"b": "r = 0.99 lies inside the range and shows a very strong positive linear relationship (about 98 percent shared variance).", "c": "r = minus 0.73 is a strong negative correlation; the minus sign gives direction, not impossibility, and its strength equals that of plus 0.73.", "d": "r = 1.0 is a perfect positive correlation, every point on a rising straight line; rare with real data but a possible value at the upper limit."}}, {"q": "A related samples t-test:", "concept": "The answer is c: a related samples t-test is appropriate for the data from a within-subjects experiment. The related t-test (also called the paired, dependent or repeated-measures t-test) compares two means that come from the same participants measured twice, in two conditions or before and after a treatment, or from matched pairs. It works on difference scores: for each person it subtracts one score from the other, then tests the null hypothesis that in the population the mean difference is zero. Because each person serves as their own control, individual differences drop out of the error term, so the test is more powerful than an independent t-test on the same number of scores. Degrees of freedom equal the number of pairs minus 1. It is a parametric test: it assumes interval or ratio data and roughly normal difference scores. William Sealy Gosset published the t distribution in 1908 under the pen name \"Student\", because his employer, the Guinness brewery, did not let staff publish under their own names. Example: Meera measures the anxiety of 20 students before and after a yoga programme. The same people give both scores, so she runs a related t-test on the 20 differences, with 19 degrees of freedom. Option c is right because a within-subjects design produces exactly the paired scores this test is built for. Exam trap: option a describes a non-parametric rank test, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945), the usual alternative for related samples, which is often described as comparing medians; the t-test compares means.", "traps": {"a": "Testing whether two population medians are equal describes a non-parametric rank test, such as the Wilcoxon signed-rank test for related samples; the related t-test tests the mean of the difference scores.", "b": "Contingency tables of frequencies are analysed with chi-square (Karl Pearson, 1900), not with a t-test, which needs interval or ratio scores.", "d": "The related t-test is parametric: it estimates population means and assumes interval data with roughly normal differences; its non-parametric counterpart is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is/are the characteristics of Karl Pearson's coefficient of correlation?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three are characteristics of Karl Pearson's coefficient of correlation: it indicates the degree of a relationship, it indicates its direction, and it is regarded as a satisfactory measure of linear correlation. Karl Pearson (1896) gave an exact formula to the idea of co-relation described by Francis Galton (1888); the result, the product-moment coefficient r, runs from minus 1 to plus 1. Degree: the size of r, ignoring the sign, gives the strength of the relationship; 0 means no linear relationship, values near 1 mean a very strong one. Direction: the sign gives the direction; plus means both variables rise together, minus means one rises as the other falls. A satisfactory measure: because it uses every observation, rests on means and standard deviations, and has a known sampling distribution that allows significance testing and leads into regression, statistics texts call it the most satisfactory (ideal) measure of linear correlation. Its limits are that it detects only straight-line relationships, is affected by extreme values, assumes interval or ratio data and says nothing about cause. Example: Arjun finds r = minus 0.65 between hours of phone use and sleep quality among his classmates. The minus sign gives the direction (more phone use, worse sleep) and 0.65 gives the degree (fairly strong). Option d is right because degree, direction and being a satisfactory measure are all listed among the merits of Pearson's r. Exam trap: picking a or b alone, because each is true; the item tests whether you remember that r carries both direction and degree in one number and is also valued as a measure.", "traps": {"a": "Degree is a true property (the size of r gives strength), but picking it alone leaves out direction (the sign) and r's standing as a satisfactory measure.", "b": "Direction is a true property (the plus or minus sign), but picking it alone leaves out degree (the size of r) and its standing as a satisfactory measure.", "c": "Being a satisfactory measure is listed among r's merits, but picking it alone leaves out the two things r actually reports: degree and direction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a measure of degree of association?", "concept": "The answer is b: omega squared is a measure of the degree (strength) of association between the independent and the dependent variable. William Hays (1963) proposed omega squared as an estimate of the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that is accounted for by the independent variable in the population. It is an effect size, like eta squared and r squared. Eta squared, used with ANOVA, is the sum of squares for the effect divided by the total sum of squares; it describes the sample and slightly overestimates the population value, so omega squared, which corrects for this, is smaller and less biased. Effect sizes answer \"how big is the effect?\", while significance tests answer only \"is there probably an effect at all?\". Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested benchmarks for eta squared of about 0.01 small, 0.06 medium and 0.14 large. Example: Riya compares three teaching methods with 900 students and gets p = 0.004, but omega squared = 0.01. The effect is real but tiny: teaching method explains only about 1 percent of the variation in marks. Option b is right because omega squared expresses how strongly the two variables are associated, and it does not grow just because the sample is large. Exam trap: a small p value is often misread as a strong effect, but the p value is the probability of results this extreme if the null hypothesis is true, and with a large sample even a trivial association becomes highly significant.", "traps": {"a": "A probability (p) value is the probability of data this extreme if the null hypothesis is true; it depends heavily on sample size and does not measure how strong the association is.", "c": "A t-value is a test statistic (a mean difference divided by its standard error); it grows with sample size, so it shows significance, not degree of association.", "d": "Degrees of freedom are the number of values free to vary (for example n minus 1), set by sample size and design; they say nothing about the strength of a relationship."}}]});
